# Custom Dropdown

## Custom Dropdown

The following example shows how to create a custom dropdown for the editor.

1. Define an icon. FroalaEditor.DefineIcon('dropdownIcon', { NAME: 'magic', SVG_KEY: 'cogs' })
2. Define a dropdown button. FroalaEditor.RegisterCommand('myDropdown', {
3. Set a title. title: 'My Dropdown',
4. Mark the [button as a dropdown](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/examples/custom-dropdown/). type: 'dropdown',
5. Specify the icon for the button. If this option is not specified, the button name will be used. icon: 'dropdownIcon',
6. Options for the dropdown. options: { 'opt1': 'Option 1', 'opt2': 'Option 2' },
7. If present, the options property will be ignored.  
 It can be used to define a custom HTML for the dropdown.  
 The current context is the editor instance. html: function () { return ''; },
8. Save the dropdown action into undo stack. undo: true,
9. Focus inside the editor before callback. focus: true,
10. Refresh the button state after the callback. refreshAfterCallback: true,
11. Add a callback.  
The current context is the editor instance. callback: function (cmd, val, params) { console.log (this.html.get()); },
12. Call a function when the dropdown button state might have changed.  
The current context is the editor instance. refresh: function ($btn) { console.log (this.selection.element()); },
13. Call a function when the dropdown is shown.  
The current context is the editor instance. refreshOnShow: function ($btn, $dropdown) { console.log (this.selection.element()); }

Your final code should look like this.

```js
FroalaEditor.DefineIcon('dropdownIcon', { NAME: 'magic', SVG_KEY: 'cogs' })

FroalaEditor.RegisterCommand('myDropdown', {
  title: 'My Dropdown',
  type: 'dropdown',
  icon: 'dropdownIcon',
  options: {
    'opt1': 'Option 1',
    'opt2': 'Option 2'
  },
  undo: true,
  focus: true,
  refreshAfterCallback: true,
  callback: function (cmd, val, params) {
    console.log (this.html.get());
  },
  refresh: function ($btn) {
    console.log (this.selection.element());
  },
  refreshOnShow: function ($btn, $dropdown) {
    console.log (this.selection.element());
  }
})
```

For a working example please refer to the [Custom Dropdown Example](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/examples/custom-dropdown/)

Once a command is defined it can be included in the buttons options, such as `imageAltButtons`, `imageEditButtons`, `imageInsertButtons`, `imageSizeButtons`, `linkEditButtons`, `linkInsertButtons`, `tableColorsButtons`, `tableEditButtons`, `tableInsertButtons`, `toolbarButtons`, `toolbarButtonsMD`, `toolbarButtonsSM`, `toolbarButtonsXS`, `videoEditButtons`, `videoInsertButtons` or `videoSizeButtons`.

# Set up a headless Froala editor

Froala’s headless mode, enabled by setting `headless: true`, transforms it into an editing engine. You design every button, menu, and dialog in your own UI, and Froala manages the editing logic, generating clean, reliable HTML.

1. Your UI (buttons, menus, dialogs)
2. Froala Action API (`commands.bold()`, `lists.format()`)
3. Editable area
4. Clean HTML (`html.get()`)

## What headless mode gives you

Think of a car. The engine does the real work, and the dashboard is how you control it. A regular Froala editor ships with both. Headless mode gives you only the engine, so you can build the dashboard that fits your product.

With `headless: true`, the editable area and every content-editing method keep working exactly as before. Froala skips drawing its own toolbar, popups, and default box styling, so your design system stays in full control.

Headless editor works well in cases like these.

- Matching the editor to an existing design system, such as your own React or Vue component library.
- Placing formatting controls anywhere on the page, like a sidebar, a floating bar, or a command palette.
- Building a focused writing experience with only the few actions your users need.

**Version.** Headless mode is available in [Froala Editor 5.5.0](https://froala.com/blog/editor/new-releases/froala-v5-5-ai-image-generation-headless-mode/) and later. Check your version with `npm ls froala-editor`.

## Quick start with vanilla JavaScript

This example builds a working headless editor in one HTML file. Copy it, open it in a browser, and click the buttons.

### Load Froala

Use the CDN for a quick test, or install the package for a real project. Pin the same version in both places so your code and your assets always match.

```shell
npm install froala-editor@5.5.0
```

### Add the markup and initialize

1. Add your own toolbar. Each button carries a `data-cmd` name.
2. Initialize Froala on a `div` with `headless: true`.
3. Enable the toolbar in the `initialized` event, once the editor is ready for API calls.

**HTML**

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <link rel="stylesheet"
    href="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/froala-editor@5.5.0/css/froala_editor.min.css">
  <link rel="stylesheet"
    href="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/froala-editor@5.5.0/css/froala_style.min.css">
</head>
<body>

  <!-- Your own toolbar. Style it any way you like. -->
  <div id="my-toolbar" role="toolbar" aria-label="Formatting" hidden>
    <button type="button" data-cmd="bold">Bold</button>
    <button type="button" data-cmd="italic">Italic</button>
    <button type="button" data-cmd="ul">Bullets</button>
    <button type="button" data-cmd="center">Center</button>
    <button type="button" data-cmd="undo">Undo</button>
  </div>

  <div id="editor"><p>Start writing here.</p></div>

  <script src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/froala-editor@5.5.0/js/froala_editor.pkgd.min.js"></script>

  <script>
    const toolbar = document.querySelector('#my-toolbar');

    const editor = new FroalaEditor('#editor', {
      headless: true,

      // key: read your license key from config, never commit it

      events: {
        initialized: function () {
          toolbar.hidden = false;
          // the API is ready from here on
        }
      }
    });

    // Map each button name to a Froala Action API call.
    const actions = {
      bold:   () => editor.commands.bold(),
      italic: () => editor.commands.italic(),
      ul:     () => editor.lists.format('UL'),
      center: () => editor.align.apply('center'),
      undo:   () => editor.commands.undo()
    };

    // Keep the text selection in place when a button is pressed.
    toolbar.addEventListener('mousedown', function (e) {
      if (e.target.closest('button')) e.preventDefault();
    });

    // Save an undo step after each change so Undo and Redo can find it.
     function runAction(action) {
     action();
     editor.undo.saveStep();
     }

    toolbar.addEventListener('click', function (e) {
    const btn = e.target.closest('button[data-cmd]');
    if (!btn || !actions[btn.dataset.cmd]) return;
    const cmd = btn.dataset.cmd;
    // Undo and redo move through the history, so they run directly.
   if (cmd === 'undo' || cmd === 'redo') actions[cmd]();
   else runAction(actions[cmd]);
   });
   </script>

</body>
</html>
```

That is a complete headless editor. The packaged build (`.pkgd`) includes every plugin, which is why the list and align calls work right away.

## Build your own toolbar

Your toolbar is plain HTML that calls Froala methods. Two small habits make it feel as smooth as the built-in one.

### Keep the selection on click

When a user presses a normal button, the browser moves focus to that button and the text selection can collapse. Calling `preventDefault()` on `mousedown` keeps focus in the editor, so “Bold” applies to the words the user actually selected. Froala’s own toolbar uses the same technique. For block actions such as align, quote, and lists, Froala 5.5.0 also focuses the editor for you when the selection sits outside it. That gives every action a caret to work from.

### Save undo steps around each action

Methods under `commands.*`, such as `commands.bold()`, record their own undo steps. Other Action API calls, such as `lists.format()`, `align.apply()`, and `colors.text()`, don’t. Call `editor.undo.saveStep()` before and after those calls. Undo then returns to the state before the change, and Redo brings it back. Froala skips duplicate steps, so wrapping every action this way is safe. Typing is saved automatically after a short pause, set by the typingTimer option.

### Wait for the initialized event

Editor methods become available once the `initialized` event fires. Showing or enabling your buttons inside that event is the simplest way to guarantee every click reaches a ready editor.

### Common Action API calls

These methods edit content directly and run normally in headless mode. Use them as the building blocks for your buttons.

| BUTTON | METHOD | DESCRIPTION |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Bold** | [commands.bold()](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/docs/methods/#commands.bold) | Bold the selected text. |
| **Italic** | [commands.italic()](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/docs/methods/#commands.italic) | Italicize the selected text. |
| **Underline** | [commands.underline()](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/docs/methods/#commands.underline) | Underline the selected text. |
| **Strikethrough** | [commands.strikeThrough()](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/docs/methods/#commands.strikeThrough) | Strikethrough the selected text. |
| **Undo** | [commands.undo()](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/docs/methods/#commands.undo) | Reverses the most recent action. |
| **Redo** | [commands.redo()](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/docs/methods/#commands.redo) | Reverses a previous undo action. |
| **Clear formatting** | [commands.clearFormatting()](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/docs/methods/#commands.clearFormatting) | Removes inline formatting from the selection. |
| **Lists** | [lists.format(type)](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/docs/methods/#lists.format) | `'UL'` for bullets, `'OL'` for numbers. |
| **Alignment** | [align.apply(type)](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/docs/methods/#align.apply) | `'left'`, `'center'`, `'right'`, or `'justify'`. |
| **Quote** | [quote.apply(value)](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/docs/methods/#quote.apply) | `'increase'` or `'decrease'` the quote level. |
| **Custom style** | [format.applyStyle(prop, value)](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/docs/methods/#format.applyStyle) | For example, `format.applyStyle('color', '#0098F7')`. |
| **Insert image** | [image.insertByURL(url)](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/docs/methods/#image.insertByURL) | In headless mode, this accepts the image URL directly, so your own input field or dialog can supply it. |

**JAVASCRIPT**

```js
// Insert an image from your own URL field.
document.querySelector('#insert-image').addEventListener('click', function () {
  const url = document.querySelector('#image-url').value.trim();
  if (url) editor.image.insertByURL(url);
});

// The image loads asynchronously, so record the undo step once it lands.
editor.events.on('image.inserted', function () {
  editor.undo.saveStep();
});
```

**TypeScript.** In the 5.5.0 typings, `image.insertByURL()` is declared with zero parameters, while the runtime accepts a URL in headless mode. Cast the call, for example `(editor.image as any).insertByURL(url)`, until the typings catch up.

## Show active button states

Users expect the Bold button to look pressed when the caret sits inside bold text. `format.is(tagName)` tells you whether a tag applies at the current selection. Refresh your buttons whenever the caret moves or the content changes.

**JAVASCRIPT**

```js
function refreshButtons(editor) {
  const states = {
    bold:   editor.format.is('strong'),
    italic: editor.format.is('em')
  };

  Object.keys(states).forEach(function (cmd) {
    const btn = document.querySelector('[data-cmd="' + cmd + '"]');
    btn.classList.toggle('is-active', states[cmd]);
    btn.setAttribute('aria-pressed', String(states[cmd]));
  });
}

const editor = new FroalaEditor('#editor', {
  headless: true,
  events: {
    keyup:          function () { refreshButtons(this); },
    mouseup:        function () { refreshButtons(this); },
    contentChanged: function () { refreshButtons(this); }
  }
});
```

Inside Froala event handlers, `this` is the editor instance. Setting `aria-pressed` lets screen reader users hear the button state too.

## Style the editing area

In headless mode, Froala adds an `fr-headless` class to the editor box. That class resets Froala’s default font, color, padding, border, and minimum height, so your page CSS decides how content looks.

Target the inner `.fr-element`, which is the element users type into. Froala’s reset uses the selector `.fr-box.fr-headless .fr-element`, so give your rule equal or higher specificity. An ID selector is the simplest choice.

**CSS**

```css
/* Frame around the editor (a wrapper you own) */
.editor-frame {
  border: 1px solid #E7E9EE;
  border-radius: 10px;
}

/* Typing area. The ID beats Froala's class-based reset. */
#editor .fr-element {
  padding: 16px;
  min-height: 220px;
  font-family: inherit;
  line-height: 1.6;
}

/* Your toolbar's pressed state */
#my-toolbar button.is-active {
  background: #E5F4FD;
  color: #0272B8;
}
```

**Tip.** Wrap `#editor` in a `div class="editor-frame"` and put the border there. Your frame then stays in your own CSS, fully separate from Froala’s classes.

## Read and save content

Content APIs are identical in headless mode. Use `html.get()` to read the editor’s HTML and `html.set()` to load saved content.

**JAVASCRIPT**

```js
document.querySelector('#save').addEventListener('click', async function () {
  await fetch('/api/documents/42', {
    method: 'PUT',
    headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
    body: JSON.stringify({ html: editor.html.get() })
  });
});

// Load saved content later
editor.html.set('<p>Welcome back.</p>');
```

**Caution.** Treat editor HTML as user input. Sanitize it on your server with a maintained HTML sanitizer before you store or display it.

## See a headless editor in action

The headless rich text editor example combines everything in this guide into one working page. Try the custom toolbar in your browser, then copy the full HTML, CSS, and JavaScript into your project.

The example includes.

- **A toolbar built from data.** Each button is one line in a list, so adding a button takes a single edit.
- **Pressed button states** for bold, italic, headings, quotes, and lists.
- **An inline link form** that keeps the user’s text selection while they type a URL.
- **Undo and redo** that move one change at a time.
- **A live HTML output panel** that shows what `html.get()` returns as you edit.

[Open the headless rich text editor example](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/examples/headless-mode/), or edit it in JSFiddle from the same page.

## Use headless mode in React

The [official React wrapper](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/docs/framework-plugins/react/) passes `config` straight to Froala, so `headless: true` works the same way. Install the core and the wrapper at matching versions so npm keeps a single copy of the editor.

```shell
npm install froala-editor@5.5.0 react-froala-wysiwyg@5.5.0
```

JSX

```js
import { useMemo, useRef, useState } from 'react';
import FroalaEditorComponent from 'react-froala-wysiwyg';
import 'froala-editor/css/froala_editor.min.css';
import 'froala-editor/css/froala_style.min.css';
import 'froala-editor/js/plugins.pkgd.min.js';

const BUTTONS = [
  { label: 'Bold',    run: (ed) => ed.commands.bold() },
  { label: 'Italic',  run: (ed) => ed.commands.italic() },
  { label: 'Bullets', run: (ed) => ed.lists.format('UL') },
  { label: 'Undo',    run: (ed) => ed.commands.undo(), history: true }
];

export default function HeadlessEditor() {
  const editorRef = useRef(null);
  const [ready, setReady] = useState(false);
  const [html, setHtml] = useState('<p>Start writing here.</p>');

  // A stable config object keeps the editor from re-initializing on every render.
  const config = useMemo(() => ({
    headless: true,
    events: {
      initialized: () => setReady(true)
    }
  }), []);

const handleClick = (button) => {
  const editor = editorRef.current && editorRef.current.getEditor();
  if (!editor) return;
  // Undo and redo move through the history, so they run directly.
  if (button.history) {
    button.run(editor);
    return;
  }
  // Save an undo step after each change so Undo and Redo can find it.
  button.run(editor);
  editor.undo.saveStep();
};

  return (
    <div>
      <div className="my-toolbar" role="toolbar" aria-label="Formatting">
        {BUTTONS.map((b) => (
          <button
            key={b.label}
            type="button"
            disabled={!ready}
            onMouseDown={(e) => e.preventDefault()}
            onClick={() => handleClick(b)}
          >
            {b.label}
          </button>
        ))}
      </div>

      <FroalaEditorComponent
        ref={editorRef}
        tag="div"
        config={config}
        model={html}
        onModelChange={setHtml}
      />
    </div>
  );
}
```

The `ref` gives you the wrapper, and `getEditor()` returns the Froala instance with the full Action API. `onModelChange` keeps the latest HTML in React state, ready to save.

**Next.js.** Froala runs in the browser. Load this component with `dynamic(() => import('./HeadlessEditor'), { ssr: false })` so it renders on the client only.

Using Vue or Angular? Pass `headless: true` inside the wrapper’s config object in the same way. See the [framework guides](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/docs/framework-plugins/) for each wrapper’s setup.

## What changes in headless mode

Headless mode sorts the API into two groups. Content methods run as usual. Methods whose only job is to open Froala’s own UI return safely as no-ops, so leftover calls in shared code stay harmless.

- Action API runs normally
- UI API safe no-op
- Defaults adjusted, still configurable

| AREA | HEADLESS BEHAVIOR |
| --- | --- |
| **Toolbar** | Your app provides it. Froala’s toolbar stays detached from the page, and `toolbar.show()`, `hide()`, `showInline()`, `disable()`, and `enable()` are no-ops. |
| **Popups and modals** | `popups.show()`, `modals.show()`, and plugin methods that open one (such as `colors.showColorsPopup()`, `image.showAltPopup()`, and `table.showTableProperties()`) are no-ops. |
| **Toasts** | Always off. |
| **Floating helpers** | Quick insert, line breaker, drag handles, and image resize handles are skipped entirely. |
| **Word paste** | Pasted Word content is cleaned right away, skipping the keep or clean dialog. `wordPasteKeepFormatting` defaults to `false` and accepts your own value. |
| **Counters** | `charCounterCount` and `wordCounterCount` default to `false`. Set either to `true` to turn it on. |
| **Collaboration** | Stays inactive, even with `collabConfig` set. The `collaborative.*` action methods are no-ops. |
| **AI features** | The AI Chat panel, inline AI suggestions, and AI Improve Writing sessions stay inactive. |
| **Styling** | The `fr-headless` class resets Froala’s wrapper and content styling, so your CSS takes over. |

**Caution.** Froala reads `headless` once, at initialization. To switch modes, call `editor.destroy()` and create a new instance with the new setting.

## Reference

### OPTION **headless**

Runs the editor as a pure editing engine for your own UI.

| FIELD | TYPE | DESCRIPTION |
| --- | --- | --- |
| [headless](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/docs/options/#headless) | boolean | Optional. Defaults to `false`. Set to `true` at initialization. |

### METHODS **editor.helpers.***

Helpers for checking headless state and building headless-aware plugins.

| METHOD | RETURNS | DESCRIPTION |
| --- | --- | --- |
| [isHeadless()](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/docs/methods/#helpers.isHeadless) | boolean | `true` when the editor runs with `headless: true`. |
| [isUserOpt(key)](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/docs/methods/#helpers.isUserOpt) | boolean | `true` only when you passed the option explicitly, as opposed to a default. |
| [headlessDefault(key, value)](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/docs/methods/#helpers.headlessDefault) | void | In headless mode, sets `editor.opts[key]` to `value` unless you set that option yourself. Outside headless mode, it leaves options as they are. |
| [guardHeadlessUI(api, names)](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/docs/methods/#helpers.guardHeadlessUI) | boolean | In headless mode, replaces each named method on `api` with a no-op that returns `false`, then returns `true`. |
| [ensureHeadlessFocus()](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/docs/methods/#helpers.ensureHeadlessFocus) | void | In headless mode, focuses the editor when the selection sits outside it, so the browser places a caret in the content. |

## Make custom plugins headless-aware

If you write your [own Froala plugins](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/docs/concepts/custom/plugin/), these helpers let them behave like the built-in ones. This section is optional for most apps.

### Guard methods that open your own UI

A plugin that opens a dialog outside Froala’s popup system should switch that method off in headless mode. `guardHeadlessUI` does this in one line, and its return value tells you whether to skip building the UI.

**JAVASCRIPT**

```js
FroalaEditor.PLUGINS.myPicker = function (editor) {
  function open() {
    const dialog = document.createElement('dialog');
    dialog.textContent = 'Pick an item';
    document.body.appendChild(dialog);
    dialog.showModal();
  }

  const api = { open: open };
  editor.helpers.guardHeadlessUI(api, ['open']);

  return api;
};
```

### Give actions a caret to work from

Call `ensureHeadlessFocus()` at the start of any action that reads the selection. It focuses the editor only when needed, and it does nothing outside headless mode, so the same plugin works in both modes.

JAVASCRIPT

```js
FroalaEditor.PLUGINS.myHighlighter = function (editor) {
  function apply() {
    editor.helpers.ensureHeadlessFocus();
    editor.format.applyStyle('background-color', 'yellow');
    editor.undo.saveStep(); // one Undo removes the highlight
  }

  return { apply: apply };
};

// Call it from your own toolbar
editor.myHighlighter.apply();
```

### Change a default while respecting user settings

`headlessDefault` sets a headless-friendly default and keeps any value the integrator passed in. Froala’s character counter, word counter, and Word paste plugins use this same approach.

**JAVASCRIPT**

```js
FroalaEditor.PLUGINS.myStatusBar = function (editor) {
  function _init() {
    // Off by default in headless mode, unless the integrator turned it on.
    editor.helpers.headlessDefault('myStatusBarEnabled', false);
  }

  return { _init: _init };
};
```

## Next steps

With your headless editor running, these pages help you add more actions to your toolbar.

- [Methods reference](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/docs/methods/) lists every Action API call you can wire to a button.
- [Events reference](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/docs/events/) covers the hooks for syncing your UI with the editor.
- [Options reference](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/docs/options/) shows the settings you can combine with `headless`.

# Custom Icon

## Custom Icon

By default the editor is using SVG icons specially created to match each action. However, the default icons can be customized and you can also add your own icons.

### *Default icon templates.*

When the editor renders an icon it is using one of the templates defined. By default the editor comes with templates for SVG icons, FontAwesome fonts, text and images.

```js
FroalaEditor.ICON_TEMPLATES = {
  font_awesome: '<i class="fa fa-[NAME]" aria-hidden="true"></i>',
  font_awesome_5: '<i class="fas fa-[FA5NAME]" aria-hidden="true"></i>',
  font_awesome_5r: '<i class="far fa-[FA5NAME]" aria-hidden="true"></i>',
  font_awesome_5l: '<i class="fal fa-[FA5NAME]" aria-hidden="true"></i>',
  font_awesome_5b: '<i class="fab fa-[FA5NAME]" aria-hidden="true"></i>',
  text: '<span style="text-align: center;">[NAME]</span>',
  image: '<img src=[SRC] alt=[ALT] />',
  svg: '<svg class="fr-svg" focusable="false" viewBox="[VIEWBOX]" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><path d="[PATH]"/></svg>',
  svgMultiplePath: '<svg class="fr-svg" focusable="false" viewBox="[VIEWBOX]" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">[PATHS]</svg>',
  empty: ' '
}
```

The `svg` template draws `PATH`, or the built-in path that `SVG_KEY` names in `FroalaEditor.SVG`, such as 'star', 'cogs' or 'close'. An icon with only a `NAME` draws an empty SVG there.

### *Define a custom icon template.*

Additionally to the templates defined by default, it is possible to create your very own templates. For example, below we're defining a template for the [Material Design](https://zavoloklom.github.io/material-design-iconic-font/icons.html) font.

Text inside the square brackets will be replaced with their value at the render time.

```js
FroalaEditor.DefineIconTemplate('material_design', '<i class="zmdi zmdi-[NAME]"></i>');
```

### *Set the default icon template.*

By default the editor is using the `svg` template, set by the [`iconsTemplate`](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/docs/options/#iconsTemplate) option, but that can be changed.

```js
FroalaEditor.ICON_DEFAULT_TEMPLATE = 'material_design';
```

### *Define a custom icon.*

Below are some examples of customizing the way icons are displayed in the editor.

```html
<div id="froala-icons"></div>

<script>
  // Change the bold icon.
  FroalaEditor.DefineIcon('bold', {NAME: 'star', SVG_KEY: 'star'});

  // Define an icon called magicIcon.
  // Because no template was specified, the default one (svg) will be used, so it needs an SVG_KEY or a PATH.
  FroalaEditor.DefineIcon('magicIcon', {NAME: 'magic', SVG_KEY: 'cogs'});
  FroalaEditor.RegisterCommand('magicButton', {
    title: 'Magic',
    icon: 'magicIcon'
  });

  // Define a text icon called textIcon.
  FroalaEditor.DefineIcon('textIcon', {NAME: 'X', template: 'text'});
  FroalaEditor.RegisterCommand('textButton', {
    title: 'Text',
    icon: 'textIcon'
  });

  // Define an image icon called imageIcon.
  FroalaEditor.DefineIcon('imageIcon', {SRC: 'https://raw.githubusercontent.com/google/material-design-icons/master/png/social/share/materialicons/36dp/1x/baseline_share_black_36dp.png', ALT: 'Image button', template: 'image'});
  FroalaEditor.RegisterCommand('imageButton', {
    title: 'Image',
    icon: 'imageIcon'
  });

  // Define a font icon called smartphoneIcon that uses the Material Design font.
  // You should also make sure that you include the Material Design Iconic font in your <head>:
  // <link rel="stylesheet" type="text/css" href="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/material-design-iconic-font/2.1.2/css/material-design-iconic-font.min.css" />
  FroalaEditor.DefineIconTemplate('material_design', '<i class="zmdi zmdi-[NAME]"></i>');
  FroalaEditor.DefineIcon('smartphoneIcon', {NAME: 'smartphone', template: 'material_design'});
  FroalaEditor.RegisterCommand('smartphoneButton', {
    title: 'Smartphone',
    icon: 'smartphoneIcon'
  });

  // Initialize.
  new FroalaEditor('#froala-icons', {
    toolbarButtons: ['bold', 'magicButton', 'textButton', 'imageButton', 'smartphoneButton']
  })
</script>
```

Once a command is defined it can be included in any of options that are using buttons, such as `imageAltButtons`, `imageEditButtons`, `imageInsertButtons`, `imageSizeButtons`, `linkEditButtons`, `linkInsertButtons`, `tableColorsButtons`, `tableEditButtons`, `tableInsertButtons`, `toolbarButtons`, `toolbarButtonsMD`, `toolbarButtonsSM`, `toolbarButtonsXS`, `videoEditButtons`, `videoInsertButtons` or `videoSizeButtons`.

