# Custom Plugin

A plugin adds options, methods and behaviour to the editor. Define it on `FroalaEditor.PLUGINS`, and every editor you create after that runs it.

## Plugin Structure

A plugin is a function that gets the editor instance and returns the plugin's public methods. Load it after the editor's script, as here, or after `import FroalaEditor from 'froala-editor'`.

```js
(function (FroalaEditor) {
  // Add an option, with its default.
  Object.assign(FroalaEditor.DEFAULTS, {
    myOption: false
  });

  // Define the plugin. editor is the instance it runs in.
  FroalaEditor.PLUGINS.myPlugin = function (editor) {
    // Private, seen only inside the plugin.
    const label = 'My plugin';

    function _privateMethod() {
      console.log(label, editor.opts.myOption);
    }

    // Public, called as editor.myPlugin.publicMethod().
    function publicMethod() {
      _privateMethod();
    }

    // Runs when each editor starts.
    function _init() {
      // Read any option, your own included.
      console.log(editor.opts.myOption);

      // Listen to any event.
      editor.events.on('contentChanged', function () {
        console.log(editor.html.get());
      });
    }

    // Return _init so the editor runs it at start.
    return {
      _init: _init,
      publicMethod: publicMethod
    };
  };
})(FroalaEditor);
```

| Part | What it does |
| --- | --- |
| `FroalaEditor.DEFAULTS` | Adds your options with their defaults. Each editor reads them, and any value passed when it starts, as `editor.opts`. |
| `FroalaEditor.PLUGINS.myPlugin` | Registers the plugin under the name `myPlugin`. The editor calls the function once for each instance. |
| `_init` | Runs when each editor starts, if the returned object includes it. Without it, the plugin's methods still work, but nothing runs at start. |
| Returned methods | Become `editor.myPlugin.publicMethod()` on each editor. Functions you don't return stay private. |

Inside the plugin, `editor` has every [option](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/docs/options/), [event](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/docs/events/) and [method](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/docs/methods/) the editor has. Add event handlers with `editor.events.on` in `_init`.

Define the plugin and its defaults before you create the editor. The editor copies the defaults when it is created, so an option added later is missing from `editor.opts`.

## Turn the Plugin On and Off

Every plugin runs by default. If you set `pluginsEnabled`, the editor runs only the plugins it names, so add your plugin's name to the list. `pluginsDisabled` turns off the plugins it names.

```js
new FroalaEditor('#editor', {
  pluginsEnabled: ['image', 'link', 'myPlugin']
});
```

## Add a Toolbar Button

A button calls the plugin through `this`, the editor. Its `plugin` property ties it to the plugin, so the button stays hidden in an editor where the plugin is turned off.

```js
FroalaEditor.DefineIcon('myButton', { NAME: 'star', SVG_KEY: 'star' });
FroalaEditor.RegisterCommand('myButton', {
  title: 'My Button',
  plugin: 'myPlugin',
  callback: function () {
    this.myPlugin.publicMethod();
  }
});

new FroalaEditor('#editor', {
  myOption: true,
  toolbarButtons: [['bold', 'italic'], ['myButton']]
});
```

[Custom Button](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/docs/concepts/custom/button/)

## Make Your Plugin Work in Headless Mode

With `headless: true`, the editor draws no toolbar, popups or modals, and your own interface drives it through the API. A plugin should behave like the built-in ones there. Methods that open UI do nothing, and methods that change content keep working. The editor has five helpers for this.

| Helper | What it does |
| --- | --- |
| [`editor.helpers.isHeadless()`](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/docs/methods/#helpers.isHeadless) | Returns `true` when the editor runs with `headless: true`. |
| [`editor.helpers.guardHeadlessUI(api, methodNames)`](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/docs/methods/#helpers.guardHeadlessUI) | In headless mode, replaces the named methods on your plugin's API with methods that do nothing and return `false`. Use it for methods that open your own UI outside the editor's popups and modals. |
| [`editor.helpers.ensureHeadlessFocus()`](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/docs/methods/#helpers.ensureHeadlessFocus) | In headless mode, focuses the editor if the selection is outside it, so the browser places a caret in the content. Call it first in methods that read the selection. |
| [`editor.helpers.headlessDefault(key, value)`](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/docs/methods/#helpers.headlessDefault) | In headless mode, sets `editor.opts[key]` to `value`, unless the option was passed in when the editor started. |
| [`editor.helpers.isUserOpt(key)`](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/docs/methods/#helpers.isUserOpt) | Returns `true` only when the option was passed in, not taken from the defaults. |

Outside headless mode, `guardHeadlessUI`, `ensureHeadlessFocus` and `headlessDefault` change nothing, so the same plugin works in every editor.

```js
(function (FroalaEditor) {
  // An option for a status bar the plugin draws.
  Object.assign(FroalaEditor.DEFAULTS, {
    myStatusBarEnabled: true
  });

  FroalaEditor.PLUGINS.myHeadlessPlugin = function (editor) {
    // Opens your own dialog. This is UI, so it should not run in headless mode.
    function openPicker() {
      const dialog = document.createElement('dialog');
      dialog.textContent = 'Pick an item';
      document.body.appendChild(dialog);
      dialog.showModal();
    }

    // Changes content. This is an action, so it keeps working in headless mode.
    function highlight() {
      // Make sure there is a caret in the editor before reading the selection.
      editor.helpers.ensureHeadlessFocus();
      editor.format.applyStyle('background-color', 'yellow');
    }

    function _init() {
      // Off by default in headless mode, unless the integrator turned it on.
      editor.helpers.headlessDefault('myStatusBarEnabled', false);
    }

    const api = {
      _init: _init,
      openPicker: openPicker,
      highlight: highlight
    };

    // In headless mode, openPicker becomes a method that does nothing.
    editor.helpers.guardHeadlessUI(api, ['openPicker']);

    return api;
  };
})(FroalaEditor);
```

[Headless Editor](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/docs/concepts/custom/headless-editor/)

