# Angular

`angular-froala-wysiwyg` adds the editor to Angular as directives. It is built for Angular 19 and later, and its source is on [GitHub](https://github.com/froala/angular-froala-wysiwyg).

## Installation

```shell
npm install froala-editor angular-froala-wysiwyg
```

The wrapper depends on the exact `froala-editor` version it was released with, so keep the two at the same version.

Add the editor's styles to the `styles` list in `angular.json`.

```json
"styles": [
  "src/styles.css",
  "node_modules/froala-editor/css/froala_editor.pkgd.min.css",
  "node_modules/froala-editor/css/froala_style.min.css"
]
```

### Update

Update both packages together.

```shell
npm install froala-editor@latest angular-froala-wysiwyg@latest
```

## Add the Editor

Import `FroalaEditorModule` and `FroalaViewModule` into a standalone component, then put the `froalaEditor` directive on an element.

```ts
import { Component } from '@angular/core';
import { FroalaEditorModule, FroalaViewModule } from 'angular-froala-wysiwyg';
import 'froala-editor/js/plugins.pkgd.min.js';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-editor',
  standalone: true,
  imports: [FroalaEditorModule, FroalaViewModule],
  template: '<div [froalaEditor]="options" [(froalaModel)]="content"></div>'
})
export class EditorComponent {
  options = {
    placeholderText: 'Edit your content here',
    charCounterCount: false
  };
  content = '<p>Hello from Froala</p>';
}
```

A new app from the Angular CLI runs with `ng serve`.

```shell
npm install -g @angular/cli
ng new my-app
cd my-app
npm install froala-editor angular-froala-wysiwyg
ng serve
```

### NgModule Apps

An app that still declares its components in an `NgModule` imports the two modules there.

```ts
@NgModule({
  imports: [BrowserModule, FroalaEditorModule.forRoot(), FroalaViewModule.forRoot()],
  declarations: [AppComponent],
  bootstrap: [AppComponent]
})
export class AppModule {}
```

The Angular CLI compiles ahead of time by default, so no other setup is needed. For an Ionic app, follow the [Ionic guide](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/docs/framework-plugins/ionic/).

[How to integrate Froala with Angular](https://froala.com/blog/editor/how-to-integrate-froala-with-angular/)

## Usage

| Binding | What it does |
| --- | --- |
| `[froalaEditor]` | Starts the editor on the element, with the options you pass. |
| `[(froalaModel)]` | The content, as an HTML string, bound both ways. |
| `(froalaInit)` | Lets you start the editor yourself. See [Manual Initialization](#manual-initialization). |
| `[froalaView]` | Shows saved content. See [Displaying HTML](#displaying-html). |

### Options

`[froalaEditor]` takes any of the editor's [options](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/docs/options/). The directive copies them when the editor starts. Two more options belong to the wrapper.

| Option | Default | What it does |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `immediateAngularModelUpdate` | `false` | Updates the model each time a key is released, not only when the editor reports a change. It may slow typing. |
| `angularIgnoreAttrs` | `null` | For [special tags](#special-tags), the attributes to leave out of the model. |

### Events and Methods

Put handlers in `events`, keyed by the [event](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/docs/events/) name. Inside a handler, `this` is the editor, not your component, so write a regular function rather than an arrow function, and call any of the editor's [methods](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/docs/methods/) on it. Typing the options as `FroalaOptions` gives `this` its type.

```ts
import type { FroalaOptions } from 'froala-editor';

options: Partial<FroalaOptions> = {
  events: {
    focus: function () {
      console.log(this.selection.get());
    }
  }
};
```

### Model and Forms

`[(froalaModel)]` keeps your property and the editor in step. When the property changes from outside, the editor replaces its content and starts a new undo history. The directive is also a form control, so `formControlName` and `[(ngModel)]` work on it.

```ts
import { Component } from '@angular/core';
import { FormControl, FormGroup, ReactiveFormsModule } from '@angular/forms';
import { FroalaEditorModule, FroalaViewModule } from 'angular-froala-wysiwyg';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-post-form',
  standalone: true,
  imports: [ReactiveFormsModule, FroalaEditorModule, FroalaViewModule],
  template: `
    <form [formGroup]="form" (ngSubmit)="onSubmit()">
      <textarea [froalaEditor] formControlName="body"></textarea>
      <button type="submit">Submit</button>
    </form>
    <div [froalaView]="form.controls.body.value"></div>
  `
})
export class PostFormComponent {
  form = new FormGroup({
    body: new FormControl('<p>Draft</p>', { nonNullable: true })
  });

  onSubmit() {
    console.log(this.form.value.body);
  }
}
```

Disabling the form control doesn't lock the editor, because the directive's `setDisabledState` does nothing. Call `edit.off()` on the editor instead.

To wrap the directive in a component of your own that works with reactive forms, see `froala.component.ts` in the repository's demo.

## Plugins and Languages

Import them from `froala-editor` in the component that shows the editor.

```ts
// Every plugin in one file. Track Changes is not in it.
import 'froala-editor/js/plugins.pkgd.min.js';

// Or one plugin at a time.
import 'froala-editor/js/plugins/align.min.js';

// A language, then language: 'de' in the options.
import 'froala-editor/js/languages/de.js';

// A third-party plugin. Add its stylesheet to angular.json.
import 'froala-editor/js/third_party/embedly.min.js';
```

- [Plugins](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/docs/plugins/)
- [Languages](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/languages/)

### Font Awesome

The editor draws its icons as SVG and doesn't need Font Awesome. Install it for the Font Awesome plugin, or to draw the toolbar icons with it (`iconsTemplate: 'font_awesome'`).

```shell
npm install font-awesome
```

```json
"styles": [
  "node_modules/font-awesome/css/font-awesome.min.css",
  "node_modules/froala-editor/css/third_party/font_awesome.min.css"
]
```

Then import `froala-editor/js/third_party/font_awesome.min.js` with the other plugins.

## Server-Side Rendering

The directive starts the editor only in the browser and loads `froala-editor` there. Load plugins and languages the same way, behind `isPlatformBrowser`, so the server never runs them.

```ts
import { Component, OnInit, PLATFORM_ID, inject } from '@angular/core';
import { isPlatformBrowser } from '@angular/common';
import { FroalaEditorModule, FroalaViewModule } from 'angular-froala-wysiwyg';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-editor',
  standalone: true,
  imports: [FroalaEditorModule, FroalaViewModule],
  template: '<div [froalaEditor]>Hello, Froala!</div>'
})
export class EditorComponent implements OnInit {
  private platformId = inject(PLATFORM_ID);

  ngOnInit() {
    if (isPlatformBrowser(this.platformId)) {
      // @ts-ignore
      import('froala-editor/js/plugins.pkgd.min.js');
      // @ts-ignore
      import('froala-editor/js/languages/de.js');
    }
  }
}
```

[Lazy loading Froala in Angular](https://froala.com/blog/general/be-lazy-the-angular-way-lazy-loading-froala-in-angular/)

## Special Tags

On `img`, `button`, `input` and `a`, the model is an object of the element's attributes, and it follows the attributes as the user edits them. An `innerHTML` key sets what is inside the element, such as a button's label. Import the plugins first, as the README says.

```ts
@Component({
  selector: 'app-banner',
  standalone: true,
  imports: [FroalaEditorModule],
  template: `
    <img [froalaEditor] [(froalaModel)]="image" />
    <button [froalaEditor]="buttonOptions" [(froalaModel)]="button"></button>
  `
})
export class BannerComponent {
  image = { src: 'path/to/image.jpg' };
  button = { innerHTML: 'Click me' };
  buttonOptions = { angularIgnoreAttrs: ['class', 'id'] };
}
```

### Ignored Attributes

`angularIgnoreAttrs` lists the attributes to leave out of the model, `null` by default.

## Manual Initialization

With `(froalaInit)` bound, the directive doesn't start the editor. It emits an object of three methods.

| Method | What it does |
| --- | --- |
| `initialize` | Starts the editor. |
| `destroy` | Destroys the editor. |
| `getEditor` | Returns the editor, or nothing before it starts. |

```ts
@Component({
  selector: 'app-editor',
  standalone: true,
  imports: [FroalaEditorModule],
  template: `
    <button (click)="controls.initialize()">Edit</button>
    <button (click)="clear()">Clear</button>
    <div [froalaEditor] (froalaInit)="onInit($event)">Click Edit to start.</div>
  `
})
export class EditorComponent {
  controls: any;

  onInit(controls: any) {
    this.controls = controls;
  }

  clear() {
    this.controls.getEditor().html.set('');
  }
}
```

## Displaying HTML

`[froalaView]` shows saved content with the editor's styles. It adds the `fr-view` class to its element and sets the HTML inside it.

```html
<div [froalaEditor] [(froalaModel)]="content"></div>
<div [froalaView]="content"></div>
```

`[froalaView]` writes the HTML to `innerHTML` as it is, past Angular's sanitizer. Clean content from users you don't trust before you show it.

## Custom Buttons

Define the button's icon and command before the editor starts, in `ngOnInit`, then name the command in `toolbarButtons`.

```ts
import { Component, OnInit } from '@angular/core';
import FroalaEditor from 'froala-editor';
import { FroalaEditorModule } from 'angular-froala-wysiwyg';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-editor',
  standalone: true,
  imports: [FroalaEditorModule],
  template: '<div [froalaEditor]="options" [(froalaModel)]="content"></div>'
})
export class EditorComponent implements OnInit {
  content = '';
  options = {
    toolbarButtons: ['paragraphFormat', 'bold', 'italic', 'underline', 'alert']
  };

  ngOnInit() {
    FroalaEditor.DefineIcon('alert', { NAME: 'info', SVG_KEY: 'help' });
    FroalaEditor.RegisterCommand('alert', {
      title: 'Hello',
      focus: false,
      undo: false,
      refreshAfterCallback: false,
      callback: function () {
        alert(this.html.get());
      }
    });
  }
}
```

