# Gatsby

Gatsby sites are React apps, so the editor comes in through the React wrapper, `react-froala-wysiwyg`, which works with React 0.14 to 19. The [React guide](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/docs/framework-plugins/react/) covers its props, events and other components.

## Installation

```shell
npm install froala-editor react-froala-wysiwyg
```

The wrapper depends on the exact `froala-editor` version it was released with, so keep the two at the same version.

## Add the Editor

Gatsby renders every page in Node when it builds the site, where there is no browser. Load the editor's plugins in the browser first, then the editor component, so the build never runs them.

```jsx
// src/components/editor.js
import React, { lazy, Suspense, useEffect, useState } from 'react';
import 'froala-editor/css/froala_style.min.css';
import 'froala-editor/css/froala_editor.pkgd.min.css';

const FroalaEditorComponent = lazy(() => import('react-froala-wysiwyg'));

export default function Editor({ initial }) {
  const [ready, setReady] = useState(false);
  const [model, setModel] = useState(initial);

  useEffect(() => {
    import('froala-editor/js/plugins.pkgd.min.js').then(() => setReady(true));
  }, []);

  return ready && (
    <Suspense fallback={<p>Loading the editor</p>}>
      <FroalaEditorComponent tag="textarea" model={model} onModelChange={setModel} />
    </Suspense>
  );
}
```

Use it on any page.

```jsx
// src/pages/index.js
import React from 'react';
import Editor from '../components/editor';

export default function IndexPage() {
  return <Editor initial="<p>Hello from Froala</p>" />;
}
```

On the server and on the first render in the browser, the component renders nothing, so the page hydrates cleanly. Options, events and the model work as in the [React guide](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/docs/framework-plugins/react/).

## Build and Serve

Build the site and serve the result with the Gatsby CLI.

```shell
npm install -g gatsby-cli
gatsby build
gatsby serve
```

