# Knockout

[GitHub](https://github.com/froala/knockout-froala)

## Install

Using Bower run.

```shell
bower install knockout-froala
```

Using npm run.

```shell
npm install froala-editor knockout knockout-froala
```

## Usage

To enable Froala binding on a text area, you need to provide the following binding handlers.

| `froala` | The model observable behind the editor |
| --- | --- |
| `froalaOptions` | A plain object or an observable that will hold all the options to initalize the editor |
| `froalaInstance` | [ optional ] meant to store the froala instance in this observable once initialized ( should be observable ). In knockout-froala 5.5.0 it stops the binding with the error "$el is not defined" before the editor starts, so leave it out. |

```js
var viewModel = {
  comments: ko.observable(),
  options: {
    // disable wrapping content with paragraphs
    // instead <div> will be used
    enter: FroalaEditor.ENTER_DIV,

    // we like gray!
    theme: 'gray',
    toolbarButtons: [ 'bold', 'italic', 'underline' ]
  }
}

ko.applyBindings( viewModel );
```

For a `<textarea>` use the following.

```html
<textarea
 data-bind="value: comments, froala: comments, froalaOptions: options">
</textarea>
```

For a `<div>` use the following.

```html
<div data-bind="froala: comments, froalaOptions: options"></div>
```

## Using knockout-froala with RequireJS

The following steps show how to use knockout-froala with RequireJS.

1. Define a list of Froala Plugins. var fe_plugins = [ 'align', 'char_counter', 'code_view', 'colors', 'draggable', 'emoticons', 'entities', 'file', 'font_family', 'font_size', 'fullscreen', 'image_manager', 'image', 'inline_style', 'line_breaker', 'link', 'lists', 'paragraph_format', 'paragraph_style', 'quote', 'save', 'table', 'url', 'video' ]
2. Define the paths for all the resources. var paths = { 'app': '../app', // The plugins ask for the editor as 'froala-editor'. 'froala-editor': '../../../bower_components/froala-wysiwyg-editor/js/froala_editor.min', 'knockout':'../../../bower_components/knockout/dist/knockout.debug', 'knockout-froala':'../../../src/knockout-froala' }; // Add Froala Editor plugins to path. for (var i = 0; i < fe_plugins.length; i++) { paths['fe_' + fe_plugins[i]] = '../../../bower_components/froala-wysiwyg-editor/js/plugins/' + fe_plugins[i] + '.min'; }
3. In the `shim` variable define dependecies among js files. var shim = {}; for (var i = 0; i < fe_plugins.length; i++) { shim['fe_' + fe_plugins[i]] = { deps: ['froala-editor'] } }
4. Init RequireJS. requirejs.config({ 'baseUrl': 'js/lib', 'paths': paths, shim: shim });
5. Load the main app module to start the app requirejs(["app"]);

The resulting code should look like this.

```js
var fe_plugins = [
  'align', 'char_counter', 'code_view', 'colors', 'draggable', 'emoticons',
  'entities', 'file', 'font_family', 'font_size', 'fullscreen',
  'image_manager', 'image', 'inline_style', 'line_breaker',
  'link', 'lists', 'paragraph_format', 'paragraph_style', 'quote',
  'save', 'table', 'url', 'video'
]

var paths = {
  'app': '../app',
  // The plugins ask for the editor as 'froala-editor'.
  'froala-editor': '../../../bower_components/froala-wysiwyg-editor/js/froala_editor.min',
  'knockout':'../../../bower_components/knockout/dist/knockout.debug',
  'knockout-froala':'../../../src/knockout-froala'
};

for (var i = 0; i < fe_plugins.length; i++) {
  paths['fe_' + fe_plugins[i]] = '../../../bower_components/froala-wysiwyg-editor/js/plugins/' + fe_plugins[i] + '.min';
}

var shim = {};

for (var i = 0; i < fe_plugins.length; i++) {
  shim['fe_' + fe_plugins[i]] = {
    deps: ['froala-editor']
  }
}

requirejs.config({
  'baseUrl': 'js/lib',
  'paths': paths,
  shim: shim
});

requirejs(["app"]);
```

`app.js` contains the logic of your app. The following example shows the code for `app.js`.

```js
requirejs(["knockout"], function(ko) {
  window.ko=ko;

  requirejs(["froala-editor"], function(FroalaEditor) {
    window.FroalaEditor = FroalaEditor;

    requirejs(["knockout-froala"], function() {

      requirejs(["fe_image","fe_char_counter"], function() {

        (function() {
          var viewModel = {
            html: ko.observable( '' ),
            options: {
              enter: FroalaEditor.ENTER_DIV,
              theme: 'gray',
              charCounterMax:150
            }
          }

          ko.applyBindings( viewModel, document.getElementById( 'app') );
        })();

      })

    })

  })
})
```

A Requirejs demo app is included in the [knockout-froala repository](https://github.com/froala/knockout-froala). You can refer to it for more details.

# React

`react-froala-wysiwyg` renders the editor as a React component. It works with React 0.14 to 19, and its source is on [GitHub](https://github.com/froala/react-froala-wysiwyg/).

## Installation

```shell
npm install froala-editor react-froala-wysiwyg
```

The wrapper depends on the exact `froala-editor` version it was released with, so keep the two at the same version.

In a Vite 8 app, set one option in `vite.config.ts` first, or the page stays blank. [Configure Vite](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/docs/framework-plugins/react/vite-typescript/#configure-vite) shows it.

- [React with Vite and TypeScript](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/docs/framework-plugins/react/vite-typescript/)
- [React Recipes](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/docs/framework-plugins/react/recipes/)

### Update

Update both packages together.

```shell
npm install froala-editor@latest react-froala-wysiwyg@latest
```

## Usage

Import the editor's styles and the plugins you want, then render `FroalaEditorComponent`.

```jsx
import { useState } from 'react';
import 'froala-editor/css/froala_style.min.css';
import 'froala-editor/css/froala_editor.pkgd.min.css';
import 'froala-editor/js/plugins.pkgd.min.js';
import FroalaEditorComponent from 'react-froala-wysiwyg';

const config = {
  placeholderText: 'Edit your content here',
  charCounterCount: false
};

export default function Editor() {
  const [model, setModel] = useState('<p>Hello from Froala</p>');

  return (
    <FroalaEditorComponent
      tag="textarea"
      config={config}
      model={model}
      onModelChange={setModel}
    />
  );
}
```

| Prop | What it does |
| --- | --- |
| `tag` | The element the editor starts on, `div` by default. For `a`, `button`, `img` and `input`, use the [special tag components](#special-tags). |
| `config` | The editor's options and events. |
| `model` | The content, as an HTML string. |
| `onModelChange` | Called with the new content each time it changes. |
| `onManualControllerReady` | Lets you start the editor yourself. See [Manual Instantiation](#manual-instantiation). |
| `skipReset` | Keeps the undo history when `model` changes from outside. Off by default. |

### Config

`config` takes any of the editor's [options](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/docs/options/). The editor reads it once, when it starts, so later changes to the object don't reach it. Two more options belong to the wrapper.

| Option | Default | What it does |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `immediateReactModelUpdate` | `false` | Updates the model each time a key is released, not only when the editor reports a change. It may slow typing. |
| `reactIgnoreAttrs` | `null` | For the special tag components, the attributes to leave out of the model. |

### Events and Methods

Put handlers in `config.events`, keyed by the [event](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/docs/events/) name. Inside a handler, `this` is the editor, so write a regular function rather than an arrow function, and call any of the editor's [methods](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/docs/methods/) on it. To keep the editor for later, store `this` in the `initialized` event.

Under `StrictMode` in development, the wrapper runs `initialized` more than once, the first time on an editor that is not ready. Keep that handler to storing `this`, as [StrictMode](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/docs/framework-plugins/react/vite-typescript/#strict-mode) explains.

```jsx
const config = {
  events: {
    initialized: function () {
      console.log('Ready with', this.html.get());
    },
    focus: function () {
      console.log(this.selection.get());
    }
  }
};
```

### Model

With `model` and `onModelChange` together, your state and the editor stay in step, as in the example above. Pass only `model` to set the starting content and leave your state alone. When `model` changes from outside, the editor replaces its content and starts a new undo history, unless `skipReset` is set.

To start the editor with other options, such as another theme or language, give the component a new `key`. Your state keeps the content.

## Plugins and Languages

Import them from `froala-editor` before the component renders.

```js
// Every plugin in one file. Track Changes is not in it.
import 'froala-editor/js/plugins.pkgd.min.js';

// Or one plugin at a time.
import 'froala-editor/js/plugins/align.min.js';
import 'froala-editor/js/plugins/track_changes.min.js';

// A language, then config={{ language: 'de' }}.
import 'froala-editor/js/languages/de.js';

// A third-party plugin and its stylesheet.
import 'froala-editor/js/third_party/embedly.min.js';
import 'froala-editor/css/third_party/embedly.min.css';
```

- [Plugins](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/docs/plugins/)
- [Languages](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/languages/)

### Font Awesome

The editor draws its icons as SVG and doesn't need Font Awesome. Install it for the Font Awesome plugin, or to draw the toolbar icons with it (`iconsTemplate: 'font_awesome'`).

```shell
npm install font-awesome
```

```js
import 'font-awesome/css/font-awesome.css';
import 'froala-editor/js/third_party/font_awesome.min.js';
import 'froala-editor/css/third_party/font_awesome.min.css';
```

## Displaying HTML

`FroalaEditorView` shows saved content with the editor's styles, in a `div` with the `fr-view` class, or the element its `tag` prop names. Import `froala_style.min.css` where you use it.

```jsx
import 'froala-editor/css/froala_style.min.css';
import FroalaEditorView from 'react-froala-wysiwyg/FroalaEditorView';

export default function Post({ html }) {
  return <FroalaEditorView model={html} />;
}
```

`FroalaEditorView` inserts the HTML as it is, with `dangerouslySetInnerHTML`. Clean content from users you don't trust before you show it.

## Special Tags

To edit an image, a button, an input or a link in place, use its component. Don't pass these tags to `FroalaEditorComponent`.

| Component | Element | Import from |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `FroalaEditorImg` | `img` | `react-froala-wysiwyg/FroalaEditorImg` |
| `FroalaEditorButton` | `button` | `react-froala-wysiwyg/FroalaEditorButton` |
| `FroalaEditorInput` | `input` | `react-froala-wysiwyg/FroalaEditorInput` |
| `FroalaEditorA` | `a` | `react-froala-wysiwyg/FroalaEditorA` |

Their model is an object of the element's attributes. An `innerHTML` key sets what is inside the element, such as a button's label, and the model follows when the user edits it. `reactIgnoreAttrs` in `config` leaves attributes out of the model.

```jsx
import { useState } from 'react';
import FroalaEditorImg from 'react-froala-wysiwyg/FroalaEditorImg';
import FroalaEditorButton from 'react-froala-wysiwyg/FroalaEditorButton';

const config = { reactIgnoreAttrs: ['class', 'id'] };

export default function Banner() {
  const [image, setImage] = useState({ src: 'path/to/image.jpg', width: '300px', alt: 'Old clock' });
  const [button, setButton] = useState({ innerHTML: 'Click me' });

  return (
    <>
      <FroalaEditorImg config={config} model={image} onModelChange={setImage} />
      <FroalaEditorButton config={config} model={button} onModelChange={setButton} />
    </>
  );
}
```

- [More on special tags](https://froala.com/blog/editor/tutorials/gaining-comprehensive-control-over-rich-text-editing-with-froala-react-sdk-components/)

## Manual Instantiation

With `onManualControllerReady`, the component doesn't start the editor. It calls your function once, with an object of three methods.

| Method | What it does |
| --- | --- |
| `initialize` | Starts the editor. |
| `destroy` | Destroys the editor. |
| `getEditor` | Returns the editor, or `null` before it starts. |

```jsx
import { useRef } from 'react';
import FroalaEditorComponent from 'react-froala-wysiwyg';

export default function Editor() {
  const controls = useRef(null);

  return (
    <>
      <button onClick={() => controls.current.initialize()}>Edit</button>
      <FroalaEditorComponent onManualControllerReady={c => { controls.current = c; }} />
    </>
  );
}
```

## Server-Side Rendering

The editor needs a browser. Importing it on the server fails, as it reads the page's DOM when it loads, so load it in the browser only, after its plugins and languages, with `React.lazy`. [Next.js and TanStack Start](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/docs/framework-plugins/react/recipes/#server-rendering) shows the shorter way each framework has.

```jsx
'use client';

import { lazy, Suspense, useEffect, useState } from 'react';
import 'froala-editor/css/froala_style.min.css';
import 'froala-editor/css/froala_editor.pkgd.min.css';

const FroalaEditorComponent = lazy(() => import('react-froala-wysiwyg'));

export default function Editor({ initial }) {
  const [ready, setReady] = useState(false);
  const [model, setModel] = useState(initial);

  useEffect(() => {
    import('froala-editor/js/plugins.pkgd.min.js').then(() => setReady(true));
  }, []);

  return ready && (
    <Suspense fallback={<p>Loading the editor</p>}>
      <FroalaEditorComponent model={model} onModelChange={setModel} />
    </Suspense>
  );
}
```

In the Next.js App Router, a component with state and effects like this one starts with `'use client'`, before its imports.

## Custom Buttons

Define each button's icon and command once, before the editor starts, then name the commands in `toolbarButtons`. Each inner list is a group of buttons.

```js
import FroalaEditor from 'froala-editor';

FroalaEditor.DefineIcon('clear', { NAME: 'remove', SVG_KEY: 'remove' });
FroalaEditor.RegisterCommand('clear', {
  title: 'Clear HTML',
  focus: false,
  undo: true,
  refreshAfterCallback: true,
  callback: function () {
    this.html.set('');
    this.events.focus();
  }
});

FroalaEditor.DefineIcon('insert', { NAME: 'plus', SVG_KEY: 'add' });
FroalaEditor.RegisterCommand('insert', {
  title: 'Insert HTML',
  focus: true,
  undo: true,
  refreshAfterCallback: true,
  callback: function () {
    this.html.insert('My New HTML');
  }
});

const config = {
  toolbarButtons: [['undo', 'redo'], ['bold'], ['clear', 'insert']]
};
```

