## TypeScript

Both packages ship their types. The components and props come from `react-froala-wysiwyg`, the editor's options from `froala-editor`. The wrapper types `config` and `onModelChange` loosely, so type your options as below, and give an arrow function's parameter a type, `(html: string) => …`, or strict mode stops it. A file that imports only `FroalaEditorView` or a special tag component needs the wrapper's types referenced once, as [Types](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/docs/framework-plugins/react/vite-typescript/#types) shows.

```ts
import FroalaEditorComponent from 'react-froala-wysiwyg';
import type { FroalaOptions } from 'froala-editor';

const config = {
  placeholderText: 'Start writing',
  events: {
    contentChanged() {
      console.log(this.html.get()); // this is the editor, typed.
    }
  }
} satisfies Partial<FroalaOptions>;

export default function Editor() {
  return <FroalaEditorComponent tag="textarea" config={config} />;
}
```

## Class Components

Class components take the same props.

```jsx
import React from 'react';
import FroalaEditorComponent from 'react-froala-wysiwyg';

export default class Editor extends React.Component {
  constructor(props) {
    super(props);
    this.config = { placeholderText: 'Edit your content here' };
    this.state = { model: 'Example text' };
    this.handleModelChange = this.handleModelChange.bind(this);
  }

  handleModelChange(model) {
    this.setState({ model });
  }

  render() {
    return (
      <FroalaEditorComponent
        tag="textarea"
        config={this.config}
        model={this.state.model}
        onModelChange={this.handleModelChange}
      />
    );
  }
}
```

| For | In a class component |
| --- | --- |
| Config | `config={this.config}` |
| Events | `events` inside `this.config` |
| Model | `model={this.state.model}` and `onModelChange={this.handleModelChange}` |
| Special tags | `<FroalaEditorImg model={this.state.image} />` |
| Manual instantiation | `onManualControllerReady={this.handleController}` |
| Displaying HTML | `<FroalaEditorView model={this.state.model} />` |

