# React Recipes

The editor in the libraries a React app's forms and data run on. Each recipe starts from an app set up as in [React with Vite and TypeScript](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/docs/framework-plugins/react/vite-typescript/), with the editor's styles and plugins imported.

## TanStack Form

Bind the editor to a field with `model` and `onModelChange`, and tell the field when the editor loses focus. Resetting the form empties the editor.

```tsx
import { useForm } from '@tanstack/react-form'
import FroalaEditorComponent from 'react-froala-wysiwyg'

const hasText = (html: string) =>
  !!new DOMParser().parseFromString(html, 'text/html').body.textContent?.trim()

export default function PostForm() {
  const form = useForm({
    defaultValues: { body: '' },
    onSubmit: async ({ value }) => console.log(value.body),
  })

  return (
    <form onSubmit={(e) => { e.preventDefault(); form.handleSubmit() }}>
      <form.Field
        name="body"
        validators={{ onSubmit: ({ value }) => (hasText(value) ? undefined : 'Write something first') }}
      >
        {(field) => (
          <>
            <FroalaEditorComponent
              model={field.state.value}
              onModelChange={(html: string) => field.handleChange(html)}
              config={{ events: { blur: () => field.handleBlur() } }}
            />
            <p role="alert">{field.state.meta.errors.join(', ')}</p>
          </>
        )}
      </form.Field>
      <button type="submit">Publish</button>
    </form>
  )
}
```

Check the text rather than the HTML. An editor the user empties reports an empty string, but one where they pressed Enter keeps empty paragraphs. If a post may be only an image, check for one too.

## React Hook Form

The editor is a controlled input, so it goes in a `Controller`. `reset()` empties it, as it does any field.

```tsx
import { Controller, useForm } from 'react-hook-form'
import FroalaEditorComponent from 'react-froala-wysiwyg'

type Post = { body: string }

const hasText = (html: string) =>
  !!new DOMParser().parseFromString(html, 'text/html').body.textContent?.trim()

export default function PostForm() {
  const { control, handleSubmit, formState: { errors } } = useForm<Post>({ defaultValues: { body: '' } })

  return (
    <form onSubmit={handleSubmit((post) => console.log(post.body))}>
      <Controller
        name="body"
        control={control}
        rules={{ validate: (html) => hasText(html) || 'Write something first' }}
        render={({ field }) => (
          <FroalaEditorComponent
            model={field.value}
            onModelChange={(html: string) => field.onChange(html)}
            config={{ events: { blur: () => field.onBlur() } }}
          />
        )}
      />
      <p role="alert">{errors.body?.message}</p>
      <button type="submit">Publish</button>
    </form>
  )
}
```

## Save With TanStack Query

Save through a mutation a second after the user stops typing. Compare with what you last saved rather than counting changes, as the editor reports a change when it starts and a count would save the draft you just loaded.

```tsx
import { useEffect, useRef, useState } from 'react'
import { useMutation } from '@tanstack/react-query'
import FroalaEditorComponent from 'react-froala-wysiwyg'

async function saveDoc(html: string) {
  const res = await fetch('/api/docs/1', {
    method: 'PUT',
    headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
    body: JSON.stringify({ html }),
  })
  if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`Save failed with ${res.status}`)
  return html
}

export default function Doc({ initial }: { initial: string }) {
  const [html, setHtml] = useState(initial)
  const saved = useRef(initial)
  const save = useMutation({ mutationFn: saveDoc, onSuccess: (html) => { saved.current = html } })
  const { mutate } = save

  useEffect(() => {
    if (html === saved.current) return
    const timer = setTimeout(() => mutate(html), 1000)
    return () => clearTimeout(timer)
  }, [html, mutate])

  return (
    <>
      <FroalaEditorComponent model={html} onModelChange={setHtml} />
      <p>{save.isPending ? 'Saving' : save.isError ? 'Not saved' : html === saved.current ? 'Saved' : 'Unsaved changes'}</p>
    </>
  )
}
```

Load the document with `useQuery` and render `Doc` once it has data, so the editor starts with the saved content.

## Upload to Your API

The editor posts each image to `imageUploadURL` and inserts the address your server answers with, as `{"link": "..."}`. `requestHeaders` goes with its requests, so the API can check who is uploading.

```tsx
import 'froala-editor/js/plugins/image.min.js'
import type { FroalaOptions } from 'froala-editor'

export const uploadConfig = (token: string) => ({
  imageUploadURL: '/api/images',
  imageUploadParam: 'file',
  requestHeaders: { Authorization: `Bearer ${token}` },
}) satisfies Partial<FroalaOptions>
```

To send the file yourself, to a signed storage address or through your app's own client, stop the editor's request in `image.beforeUpload` and insert the image when yours is done.

```tsx
export const ownUploadConfig = (token: string) => ({
  events: {
    'image.beforeUpload'(files) {
      const editor = this
      const body = new FormData()
      body.append('file', files[0])
      fetch('/api/images', { method: 'POST', headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${token}` }, body })
        .then((res) => res.json())
        .then(({ link }: { link: string }) => editor.image.insert(link, true, {}, editor.image.get()))
      return false
    },
  },
}) satisfies Partial<FroalaOptions>
```

- [Uploading Images](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/docs/concepts/image/upload/)
- [Server Integrations](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/docs/server-integrations/)

## MUI

### A Label and Helper Text

Put the editor in a `FormControl` with a `FormLabel`, and name the editing area after the label so screen readers say it.

```tsx
import { useId, useState } from 'react'
import { FormControl, FormHelperText, FormLabel } from '@mui/material'
import FroalaEditorComponent from 'react-froala-wysiwyg'
import type { FroalaOptions } from 'froala-editor'

export default function LessonField() {
  const labelId = useId()
  const [html, setHtml] = useState('')
  const error = html === '' ? 'Write the lesson first' : ''

  return (
    <FormControl error={!!error} fullWidth>
      <FormLabel id={labelId}>Lesson text</FormLabel>
      <FroalaEditorComponent
        model={html}
        onModelChange={setHtml}
        config={{
          events: {
            initialized() {
              this.el?.setAttribute('aria-labelledby', labelId)
            },
          },
        } satisfies Partial<FroalaOptions>}
      />
      <FormHelperText>{error || 'Students see this text'}</FormHelperText>
    </FormControl>
  )
}
```

### Dark Mode

With a theme made with `colorSchemes: { dark: true }`, load the editor's dark theme and follow MUI's mode. The editor reads its options once, so a new `key` starts it again in the other theme, with the content kept in your state.

```tsx
import { useColorScheme } from '@mui/material/styles'
import 'froala-editor/css/themes/dark.min.css'
import FroalaEditorComponent from 'react-froala-wysiwyg'

export default function ThemedEditor({ html, onChange }: { html: string; onChange: (html: string) => void }) {
  const { mode, systemMode } = useColorScheme()
  const dark = (mode === 'system' ? systemMode : mode) === 'dark'

  return <FroalaEditorComponent key={dark ? 'dark' : 'light'} config={{ theme: dark ? 'dark' : '' }} model={html} onModelChange={onChange} />
}
```

### In a Dialog

The editor works in a `Dialog` as it is. Its popups open inside the dialog, take the keyboard and show above it, with no change to the dialog's focus handling.

## Next.js and TanStack Start

The editor needs a browser. Importing it on the server fails, as it reads the page's DOM when it loads. In a framework that renders on the server, load your editor component in the browser only. It is the `Editor` from [Add the Editor](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/docs/framework-plugins/react/vite-typescript/#add-the-editor), with its styles and plugins.

In the Next.js App Router, load it with `next/dynamic` and `ssr: false`, from a client component.

```tsx
'use client'

import dynamic from 'next/dynamic'

const Editor = dynamic(() => import('./Editor'), { ssr: false, loading: () => <p>Loading the editor</p> })

export default function EditPost() {
  return <Editor initial="<p>Draft</p>" />
}
```

In TanStack Start, put it in `ClientOnly`. On the server it renders the fallback and never imports the editor. Start builds with Vite, so [Configure Vite](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/docs/framework-plugins/react/vite-typescript/#configure-vite) applies too.

```tsx
import { lazy, Suspense } from 'react'
import { ClientOnly } from '@tanstack/react-router'

const Editor = lazy(() => import('./Editor'))

export default function EditPost() {
  return (
    <ClientOnly fallback={<p>Loading the editor</p>}>
      <Suspense fallback={<p>Loading the editor</p>}>
        <Editor initial="<p>Draft</p>" />
      </Suspense>
    </ClientOnly>
  )
}
```

# React with Vite and TypeScript

Most new React apps start from Vite's React and TypeScript template. This guide sets the editor up in one, with typed options and events, the editor in reach of your code, the license key from the environment and the editor loaded only on the pages that use it. For every prop and option, see the [React](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/docs/framework-plugins/react/) guide.

## Create the App

Skip this in an app you already have.

```shell
npm create vite@latest my-app -- --template react-ts
cd my-app
npm install froala-editor react-froala-wysiwyg
```

## Add the Editor

Put the editor in a component of its own, with its styles, the plugins its toolbar needs and its options in one place. Import each plugin before the component that uses it, as a file of its own or all of them with `froala-editor/js/plugins.pkgd.min.js`.

```tsx
// src/Editor.tsx
import { useState } from 'react'
import 'froala-editor/css/froala_style.min.css'
import 'froala-editor/css/froala_editor.pkgd.min.css'
import 'froala-editor/js/plugins/image.min.js'
import 'froala-editor/js/plugins/link.min.js'
import 'froala-editor/js/plugins/lists.min.js'
import FroalaEditorComponent from 'react-froala-wysiwyg'
import type { FroalaOptions } from 'froala-editor'

const config = {
  key: import.meta.env.VITE_FROALA_KEY,
  placeholderText: 'Start writing',
  toolbarButtons: [['undo', 'redo'], ['bold', 'italic'], ['insertLink', 'insertImage'], ['formatUL', 'formatOL']],
} satisfies Partial<FroalaOptions>

export default function Editor({ initial = '' }: { initial?: string }) {
  const [html, setHtml] = useState(initial)
  return <FroalaEditorComponent tag="textarea" config={config} model={html} onModelChange={setHtml} />
}
```

The editor reads `config` once, when it starts. Keep it outside the component, or in `useMemo` when it needs the component's values, and give the component a new `key` to start it again with other options.

## License Key

Vite gives your code the variables in `.env` files whose names start with `VITE_`. Put the key in `.env.local`, which the template keeps out of git, and type it in a declaration file under `src`.

```shell
# .env.local
VITE_FROALA_KEY=YOUR_LICENSE_KEY
```

```ts
// src/env.d.ts
/// <reference types="react-froala-wysiwyg" />

interface ImportMetaEnv {
  readonly VITE_FROALA_KEY: string
}
```

The reference line loads the wrapper's types for every file. [Types](#types) says why.

- [Activation](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/docs/activation/)

## Types

Both packages ship their types. The editor's are full. The wrapper's are thin, so a few things need a hand.

| Where | What to write | Why |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `config` | `satisfies Partial<FroalaOptions>`, or the type on the variable | The wrapper takes any object. With the type, option values are checked and `this` in an event handler is the editor. Misspelt option names still pass, as the options type takes any name for plugins' options. |
| `onModelChange` | A state setter as it is, or `(html: string) => …` | The prop has no function type, so strict mode stops an arrow function whose parameter has none. |
| A file that imports only `FroalaEditorView` or a special tag component | The reference line from [License Key](#license-key) | Their types sit in the main entry's declaration file, which TypeScript only reads once something imports it. |

```tsx
const config = {
  heightMin: 200,
  events: {
    // this is the editor, typed.
    contentChanged() {
      console.log(this.html.get())
    },
  },
} satisfies Partial<FroalaOptions>
```

## Reach the Editor

To call the editor's [methods](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/docs/methods/) from your own buttons, keep it in a ref when it starts. The ref is typed as the editor.

```tsx
import { useMemo, useRef } from 'react'
import FroalaEditor from 'froala-editor'
import type { FroalaOptions } from 'froala-editor'
import FroalaEditorComponent from 'react-froala-wysiwyg'

export default function Reply() {
  const editor = useRef<FroalaEditor | null>(null)
  const config = useMemo(() => ({
    events: {
      initialized() {
        editor.current = this
      },
    },
  }) satisfies Partial<FroalaOptions>, [])

  return (
    <>
      <FroalaEditorComponent config={config} />
      <button onClick={() => editor.current?.html.insert('<p>Thanks for writing in.</p>')}>Insert greeting</button>
    </>
  )
}
```

A ref on the component works too, `useRef<FroalaEditorComponent>(null)` and then `ref.current?.getEditor()`, but `getEditor` returns `any`.

## StrictMode

The template wraps the app in `StrictMode`, which mounts each component twice in development. The wrapper then runs your `initialized` handler three times, the first time on an editor that is not ready, so reading or changing the content there throws. Keep the handler to keeping `this`, as above, or to setting attributes with `this.el?.`. A production build runs it once.

## Load It Where It Is Used

The editor, the wrapper and two plugins come to about 160 KB of JavaScript and 37 KB of CSS, gzipped. Load them only where someone edits, with `lazy`, and the rest of the app starts without them.

```tsx
import { lazy, Suspense } from 'react'

const Editor = lazy(() => import('./Editor'))

export default function EditPost() {
  return (
    <Suspense fallback={<p>Loading the editor</p>}>
      <Editor initial="<p>Draft</p>" />
    </Suspense>
  )
}
```

A router that splits code by route does the same for a whole page, as TanStack Router does for a route's `.lazy.tsx` file.

## Test It

The editor needs a real browser. In unit tests with Vitest and a simulated DOM, let a textarea stand in for it and test your form's logic.

```tsx
// PostForm.test.tsx
import { fireEvent, render, screen } from '@testing-library/react'
import { expect, test, vi } from 'vitest'
import PostForm from './PostForm'

vi.mock('react-froala-wysiwyg', () => ({
  default: ({ model, onModelChange }: { model?: string; onModelChange?: (html: string) => void }) => (
    <textarea aria-label="Editor" value={model ?? ''} onChange={(e) => onModelChange?.(e.target.value)} />
  ),
}))

test('asks for text before it publishes', async () => {
  render(<PostForm />)
  fireEvent.click(screen.getByText('Publish'))
  expect(await screen.findByText('Write something first')).toBeTruthy()
})
```

In a browser test, the editing area is `.fr-element`, and Playwright types into it like any field. The editor reports the change, so your form sees it.

```ts
await page.locator('.fr-element').fill('Hello from the test')
```

