# Migrate from ContentTools

ContentTools edits the regions of a page in place. This guide replaces it with Froala on the same regions, with its toolbar inline, and keeps the way you save each region.

The latest ContentTools release on npm is 1.6.16, from June 2022. Its authors plan a second version in a [roadmap repository](https://github.com/GetmeUK/contenttools-2-roadmap).

## Initialization

| Task | ContentTools | Froala |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Load it | `content-tools.min.css`, `content-tools.min.js`, and its images and icon font next to the stylesheet | `froala_editor.pkgd.min.css` and `froala_editor.pkgd.min.js`, from the CDN or npm |
| Mark what is editable | An attribute on each region, such as `data-editable` | Any element. A selector that matches several elements starts one editor on each. |
| Start it | `ContentTools.EditorApp.get().init('*[data-editable]', 'data-name')` | `new FroalaEditor('[data-editable]', { toolbarInline: true })` |

### Remove ContentTools

Remove its stylesheet and script, the files you copied next to them and the script that starts it. If you installed it with npm, uninstall the package.

```html
<!-- Remove -->
<link rel="stylesheet" type="text/css" href="assets/content-tools.min.css">
<script src="assets/content-tools.min.js"></script>
<script>
  window.addEventListener('load', function () {
    var editor = ContentTools.EditorApp.get();
    editor.init('*[data-editable]', 'data-name');
  });
</script>
```

```shell
npm uninstall ContentTools
```

Keep the `data-editable` and `data-name` attributes on your regions. Froala starts on them, and the names label the content when you save it.

### Add Froala

Add the editor's stylesheet in the `head` and its script at the end of the `body`. The editor draws its icons as SVG and needs neither Font Awesome nor jQuery.

```html
<link href="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/froala-editor@latest/css/froala_editor.pkgd.min.css" rel="stylesheet">
<script src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/froala-editor@latest/js/froala_editor.pkgd.min.js"></script>
```

With a bundler, install the package and import the same files.

```shell
npm install froala-editor
```

Start the editor on your regions. `toolbarInline` shows the toolbar next to the selected text, as an in-place editor does, and `toolbarVisibleWithoutSelection` keeps it next to the cursor.

```js
// ContentTools
var editor = ContentTools.EditorApp.get();
editor.init('*[data-editable]', 'data-name');

// Froala
var editors = new FroalaEditor('[data-editable]', {
  key: 'YOUR_LICENSE_KEY',
  toolbarInline: true
});
```

### Full Example

```html
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8">
    <title>Migrate from ContentTools to Froala</title>
    <link href="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/froala-editor@latest/css/froala_editor.pkgd.min.css" rel="stylesheet">
  </head>
  <body>
    <div data-editable data-name="main-content">
      <h2>Welcome</h2>
      <p>Select this text to edit it.</p>
    </div>

    <script src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/froala-editor@latest/js/froala_editor.pkgd.min.js"></script>
    <script>
      var editors = new FroalaEditor('[data-editable]', {
        key: 'YOUR_LICENSE_KEY',
        toolbarInline: true
      });
    </script>
  </body>
</html>
```

[Activation](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/docs/activation/)

## Save the Content

ContentTools hands you the changed regions in its `saved` event. With Froala, start one editor per region and read each one's HTML with `html.get()` when you save, under the region's `data-name`.

```js
// ContentTools
editor.addEventListener('saved', function (ev) {
  var regions = ev.detail().regions;
  var payload = new FormData();
  for (var name in regions) payload.append(name, regions[name]);
  fetch('/save-my-page', { method: 'POST', body: payload });
});

// Froala
var regions = document.querySelectorAll('[data-editable]');
var editors = Array.from(regions, function (el) {
  return new FroalaEditor(el, { key: 'YOUR_LICENSE_KEY', toolbarInline: true });
});

function save() {
  var payload = new FormData();
  regions.forEach(function (el, i) {
    payload.append(el.dataset.name, editors[i].html.get());
  });
  fetch('/save-my-page', { method: 'POST', body: payload });
}
```

To save each region on its own, as the user types or on demand, use the Save plugin and its `saveURL` option.

[Save plugin](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/docs/plugins/save-plugin/)

## Match the Features

| ContentTools | Froala |
| --- | --- |
| An upload function you set as `ContentTools.IMAGE_UPLOADER` | The Image plugin. Set `imageUploadURL`, and your server returns JSON with a `link` field. Users can also drop or paste images, insert them by URL, resize them and add captions. |
| Styles added with `ContentTools.StylePalette.add` | `paragraphStyles` for paragraphs and `inlineClasses` for selected text, each a class you define in your CSS with its label |
| Editing in place on the page | `toolbarInline: true` |

```js
// ContentTools
ContentTools.StylePalette.add([
  new ContentTools.Style('Author', 'author', ['p'])
]);

// Froala
new FroalaEditor('[data-editable]', {
  toolbarInline: true,
  imageUploadURL: '/upload_image',
  paragraphStyles: { author: 'Author' }
});
```

- [Feature matching](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/docs/migration-guides/contenttools/feature-matching/)
- [Image upload](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/docs/concepts/image/upload/)
- [All options](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/docs/options/)

# Migrate ContentTools Options

## Options

The following guide explains how to pass different Froala Editor options and highlights what to do when migrating from ContentTools.



### 1\. Running the code

ContentTools can be used only for elements that have “data-editable” tags, in contrast Froala can be loaded on any element. Both of them use a DOM selector to fetch the element that includes the editor. The example below, uses a div tag to get the containing element. With ContentTools you must use a block level HTML element like “div” but with Froala you can use other DOM elements such as textarea or span as the target elements in your page.

ContentTools

```
<div data-editable data-name="main-content">
  <p>John F. Woods</p>
</div>

<script src="assets/content-tools.min.js"></script>

<script>
  window.addEventListener('load', function() {
    var editor;
    editor = ContentTools.EditorApp.get();
    editor.init('div[data-editable]', 'data-name');
  });
</script>
```

Froala Editor

```
<div id="main-content">
  <p>John F. Woods</p>
</div>

const editor = new FroalaEditor('#main-content);
```



Froala can be initiated on an image using the image.min.js plugin

```
<!-- HTML -->
<img id="edit" src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/froala/wysiwyg-editor/master/editor.jpg" alt="Old Clock" width="300"/>

<!-- Javascript -->
<script>
  new FroalaEditor('img#edit');
</script>
```



It is possible to initialize the Froala Editor on click on an element

```
<!-- HTML -->
<div id="editor">Hello ContentTools</div>

<!-- Javascript -->
<script>
  new FroalaEditor('#editor', {
    initOnClick: true
  });
</script>
```



### 2\. Working with plugins

ContentTools uses Tools to customize its behavior and functionality, Forala uses Plugins to do the same. Froala Editor has over 30 plugins, you can specify the required plugins at initialization. .

**Note:** By default, all plugins are enabled without using the pluginsEnabled option. Also, each plugin requires you to include the corresponding JS and CSS files. Here is the [complete list of plugins](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/docs/plugins/) and the files required by them.

ContentTools

```
window.addEventListene ('load', function() {
  var editor;
  editor = ContentTools.EditorApp.get();

  //init contentTools
  editor.init('*[data-editable]', 'data-name');

  toolbox = editor.toolbox();

  //enabled tools
  tools = [
    [
      'bold',
      'italic',
      'link',
      'align-left',
      'align-center',
      'align-right'
    ], 
    [
      'image',
      'video',
      'preformatted'
    ], 
  ];

  //set tools
  toolbox.tools(tools);
});
```

Froala Editor

```
new FroalaEditor('#editor', {
  pluginsEnabled: ['image', 'link']
});
```



If you do not find the plugin you want, you can [create your own](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/docs/concepts/custom/plugin/).



### 3\. Toolbar Customizations

In ContentTools the tools you enable are responsible for displaying buttons on the toolbar, Froala Editor has a separate option that enables you to customize the toolbar. It allows you not only to choose how to group buttons but also to choose them based on screen resolution, this allows the editor to display properly on all devices. Refer to [Options](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/docs/options/) to find a complete list of available options.

ContentTools toolbar customizations

```
window.addEventListener('load', function() {
  var editor;
  editor = ContentTools.EditorApp.get();

  //init contentTools
  editor.init('*[data-editable]', 'data-name');

  toolbox = editor.toolbox();

  //enabled tools
  tools = [
    [
      'bold',
      'italic',
      'link',
      'align-left',
      'align-center',
      'align-right'
    ]
  ];

  //set tools
  toolbox.tools(tools);
});
```

Froala Editor

```
<script>
  new FroalaEditor('#editor', {
    pluginsEnabled: ['image', 'link','Table'],
    toolbarButtons: [
      'undo', 'redo', '|', 'bold', 'italic', '|', 'insertLink', 'insertImage', '-',
      'alignLeft', 'alignCenter', 'alignRight'
    ]
  });
</script>
```

