# Migrate from ProseMirror

This guide maps a ProseMirror setup, built from `prosemirror-state`, `prosemirror-view` and `prosemirror-model`, to Froala, step by step, with the code for both.

## Before You Start

ProseMirror is a toolkit. You define a `Schema` of the nodes and marks a document may hold, create an `EditorState`, render it with an `EditorView` and apply every change as a `Transaction`. The toolbar, the keymap and undo are plugins you add. The document is a tree of nodes, and you serialize it to HTML when you need HTML.

Froala starts as a complete editor, with its toolbar, plugins and image handling, on the element you name. Its document is HTML, so `editor.html.get()` and `editor.html.set()` read and write it.

| Concept | ProseMirror | Froala |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Start an editor | `new EditorView(mount, { state })` | `new FroalaEditor('#editor', options)` |
| Where it runs | Inside the mount element you pass to the view | On the element a CSS selector or a DOM node names. A selector that matches several elements starts one editor on each. |
| What the document may hold | The `Schema` | `htmlAllowedTags` and `htmlAllowedAttrs` |
| Features | A `plugins` array you compose, such as history and keymap | Plugins loaded from one file, picked with `pluginsEnabled` |
| Toolbar | `prosemirror-menu`, or the menu bar of `prosemirror-example-setup` | `toolbarButtons`, a list of button groups |
| Read content | `DOMSerializer` on `view.state.doc`, or `doc.toJSON()` | `editor.html.get()` |
| Write content | `DOMParser`, then a transaction that replaces the document | `editor.html.set(html)` |
| React to changes | The `dispatchTransaction` prop, or plugin props | Named events in `events`, such as `contentChanged` |
| Remove the editor | `view.destroy()` | `editor.destroy()` |
| License | MIT, open source | Commercial, activated with the `key` option |

The main change is the model. There is no schema, state or transaction to keep in step. You start the editor on an element with an options object, and read or write its HTML.

## Replace ProseMirror in Three Steps

### Remove ProseMirror

Delete the ProseMirror imports and the state and view setup, then remove the packages you installed. A typical setup uses these.

```shell
npm uninstall prosemirror-state prosemirror-view prosemirror-model prosemirror-schema-basic prosemirror-schema-list prosemirror-example-setup prosemirror-commands prosemirror-keymap prosemirror-history prosemirror-menu
```

### Add Froala

Install the package for a bundled app, or load the packaged files from the CDN. The packaged script holds the editor and every plugin in `js/plugins` except Track Changes and Trim Video.

```shell
npm install froala-editor
```

```html
<link href="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/froala-editor@latest/css/froala_editor.pkgd.min.css" rel="stylesheet">
<script src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/froala-editor@latest/js/froala_editor.pkgd.min.js"></script>
```

### Start the Editor

ProseMirror needs a schema, a state and a view before anything is editable, and its basic example parses the starting content from a separate element. Froala needs one call, and the HTML already in the element becomes its content. Add your license key here.

```js
// ProseMirror
import { EditorState } from 'prosemirror-state';
import { EditorView } from 'prosemirror-view';
import { DOMParser } from 'prosemirror-model';
import { schema } from 'prosemirror-schema-basic';
import { exampleSetup } from 'prosemirror-example-setup';

const view = new EditorView(document.querySelector('#editor'), {
  state: EditorState.create({
    doc: DOMParser.fromSchema(schema).parse(document.querySelector('#content')),
    plugins: exampleSetup({ schema })
  })
});

// Froala
import FroalaEditor from 'froala-editor';
import 'froala-editor/css/froala_editor.pkgd.min.css';
import 'froala-editor/js/plugins.pkgd.min.js';

const editor = new FroalaEditor('#editor', {
  key: 'YOUR_LICENSE_KEY'
});
```

[Activation](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/docs/activation/)

## Configuration

In ProseMirror, behaviour comes from the plugins you compose and the props of the view. In Froala, it is one flat options object.

| ProseMirror | Froala |
| --- | --- |
| A placeholder plugin you write, as no ProseMirror package has one | `placeholderText` |
| `attributes: { style: 'min-height: 200px' }` on the view | `heightMin: 200` |
| `exampleSetup({ schema, menuBar: true })`, or `prosemirror-menu` | `toolbarButtons` |

```js
// ProseMirror
import { placeholder } from './placeholder-plugin'; // your own plugin

const view = new EditorView(mount, {
  state: EditorState.create({
    schema,
    plugins: [...exampleSetup({ schema, menuBar: true }), placeholder('Start typing')]
  }),
  attributes: { style: 'min-height: 200px' }
});

// Froala
const editor = new FroalaEditor('#editor', {
  key: 'YOUR_LICENSE_KEY',
  placeholderText: 'Start typing',
  heightMin: 200,
  toolbarButtons: [
    ['undo', 'redo'],
    ['bold', 'italic', 'underline'],
    ['insertLink', 'insertImage'],
    ['formatUL', 'formatOL']
  ]
});
```

[All options](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/docs/options/)

## Plugins

ProseMirror plugins are objects you create and list in the state's `plugins` array, history and keymaps included.

In Froala, undo, redo and the common keyboard shortcuts are part of the core editor, and `shortcutsEnabled` lists the shortcuts that are on. Every loaded plugin is on by default. `pluginsEnabled` keeps only the plugins it names, and `pluginsDisabled` turns off the ones it names.

```js
// ProseMirror
import { history, undo, redo } from 'prosemirror-history';
import { keymap } from 'prosemirror-keymap';
import { baseKeymap } from 'prosemirror-commands';

const state = EditorState.create({
  schema,
  plugins: [history(), keymap({ 'Mod-z': undo, 'Mod-y': redo }), keymap(baseKeymap)]
});

// Froala, only these plugins
new FroalaEditor('#editor', {
  pluginsEnabled: ['align', 'lists', 'link', 'image', 'table']
});

// Froala, every plugin but these
new FroalaEditor('#editor', {
  pluginsDisabled: ['video', 'emoticons']
});
```

[Plugins](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/docs/plugins/)

## Get and Set Content

ProseMirror keeps a node tree, so HTML goes through a serializer one way and a parser and a transaction the other. Froala keeps HTML, so each is one call.

### Read

```js
// ProseMirror
import { DOMSerializer } from 'prosemirror-model';

function getHTML(view) {
  const fragment = DOMSerializer.fromSchema(view.state.schema).serializeFragment(view.state.doc.content);
  const div = document.createElement('div');
  div.appendChild(fragment);
  return div.innerHTML;
}

const json = view.state.doc.toJSON(); // or the document as JSON

// Froala
const html = editor.html.get();
```

### Write

```js
// ProseMirror
import { DOMParser } from 'prosemirror-model';

function setHTML(view, html) {
  const tmp = document.createElement('div');
  tmp.innerHTML = html;
  const doc = DOMParser.fromSchema(view.state.schema).parse(tmp);
  view.dispatch(view.state.tr.replaceWith(0, view.state.doc.content.size, doc.content));
}

// Froala
editor.html.set('<p>Hello <strong>Froala</strong></p>');
```

## Events

ProseMirror reports changes through `dispatchTransaction`, where you apply each transaction yourself. Froala has named events, set in the `events` option or later with `editor.events.on()`. The one for a changed document is `contentChanged`. Inside a handler, `this` is the editor, so write a regular function rather than an arrow function.

```js
// ProseMirror
const view = new EditorView(mount, {
  state,
  dispatchTransaction(tr) {
    view.updateState(view.state.apply(tr));
    if (tr.docChanged) console.log('Document changed');
  }
});

// Froala
new FroalaEditor('#editor', {
  events: {
    initialized: function () {
      console.log('Editor ready');
    },
    contentChanged: function () {
      console.log('Document changed', this.html.get());
    }
  }
});
```

[All events](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/docs/events/)

## Methods

ProseMirror makes a view read-only through its `editable` prop, which you change with `setProps`. Froala has a method for each action. Call them once the editor has started, in the `initialized` event or later.

| Action | ProseMirror | Froala |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Read-only | `view.setProps({ editable: () => false })` | `editor.edit.off()` |
| Editable again | `view.setProps({ editable: () => true })` | `editor.edit.on()` |
| Is it read-only | `view.editable` | `editor.edit.isDisabled()` |
| Focus | `view.focus()` | `editor.events.focus()` |
| Remove the editor | `view.destroy()` | `editor.destroy()` |

[All methods](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/docs/methods/)

## Image Upload

ProseMirror leaves uploads to you. You add an image node to the schema, catch the paste or drop, upload the file and dispatch a transaction that inserts the node.

Froala's Image plugin selects, uploads and inserts the image. Set `imageUploadURL`, and the editor sends the file in the `file` field of a POST request. Your server returns JSON with a `link` field holding the image's address.

```js
// ProseMirror
const view = new EditorView(mount, {
  state,
  handlePaste(view, event) {
    const file = event.clipboardData?.files?.[0];
    if (!file) return false;
    uploadFile(file).then(url => {
      const node = view.state.schema.nodes.image.create({ src: url });
      view.dispatch(view.state.tr.replaceSelectionWith(node));
    });
    return true;
  }
});

async function uploadFile(file) {
  const body = new FormData();
  body.append('file', file);
  const res = await fetch('/upload', { method: 'POST', body });
  return (await res.json()).link;
}

// Froala
new FroalaEditor('#editor', {
  imageUploadURL: '/upload',
  imageUploadParams: { folder: 'posts' },
  events: {
    'image.uploaded': function (response) {
      console.log('Uploaded', response);
    },
    'image.error': function (error, response) {
      console.error('Upload failed', error.message);
    }
  }
});
```

[Image Upload](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/docs/concepts/image/upload/)

## React

ProseMirror's own packages include no React component, so a common pattern creates the `EditorView` in a `useEffect`, keeps the mount element in a ref and destroys the view on cleanup.

Froala's React component is `react-froala-wysiwyg`. It binds the content with `model` and `onModelChange`.

```shell
npm install froala-editor react-froala-wysiwyg
```

```jsx
// ProseMirror
import { useEffect, useRef } from 'react';
import { EditorState } from 'prosemirror-state';
import { EditorView } from 'prosemirror-view';
import { schema } from 'prosemirror-schema-basic';
import { exampleSetup } from 'prosemirror-example-setup';

export function Editor() {
  const ref = useRef(null);
  useEffect(() => {
    const view = new EditorView(ref.current, {
      state: EditorState.create({ schema, plugins: exampleSetup({ schema }) })
    });
    return () => view.destroy();
  }, []);
  return <div ref={ref} />;
}

// Froala
import { useState } from 'react';
import 'froala-editor/css/froala_style.min.css';
import 'froala-editor/css/froala_editor.pkgd.min.css';
import 'froala-editor/js/plugins.pkgd.min.js';
import FroalaEditorComponent from 'react-froala-wysiwyg';

const config = { key: 'YOUR_LICENSE_KEY' };

export function Editor() {
  const [model, setModel] = useState('');
  return (
    <FroalaEditorComponent tag="textarea" config={config} model={model} onModelChange={setModel} />
  );
}
```

[React guide](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/docs/framework-plugins/react/)

