# .NET Image S3 Upload

Send images from the editor straight to an Amazon S3 bucket. Your server signs the upload with `FroalaEditor.S3.GetHash`, and the AWS secret key stays on the server.

## How It Works

1. You create a bucket on Amazon S3 and give it a CORS rule.
2. Your server computes a signed upload policy.
3. The page fetches the signed policy and starts the editor with it, in the `imageUploadToS3` option.
4. Images go from the browser to the bucket, without passing through your server.

## Create an S3 Bucket

Amazon's guide to [creating a bucket](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/create-bucket-overview.html) covers the name and the region. Note both, the signature needs them.

New buckets have ACLs turned off, and they turn down uploads that ask for an ACL such as `public-read`. Either turn ACLs on for the bucket, or set `Acl` to `bucket-owner-full-control` and let the public read the images through a bucket policy.

## Set CORS on the S3 Bucket

A CORS rule tells S3 which sites may send it requests. In the bucket's Permissions tab, paste this rule, with your site's address in place of `ALLOWED_URL`. The S3 console takes the rule as JSON, and the S3 API also takes it as XML. Amazon's [CORS guide](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/cors.html) explains each field.

```json
[
  {
    "AllowedOrigins": ["ALLOWED_URL"],
    "AllowedMethods": ["GET", "POST", "PUT"],
    "AllowedHeaders": ["*"],
    "MaxAgeSeconds": 3000
  }
]
```

```xml
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<CORSConfiguration xmlns="http://s3.amazonaws.com/doc/2006-03-01/">
  <CORSRule>
    <AllowedOrigin>ALLOWED_URL</AllowedOrigin>
    <AllowedMethod>GET</AllowedMethod>
    <AllowedMethod>POST</AllowedMethod>
    <AllowedMethod>PUT</AllowedMethod>
    <MaxAgeSeconds>3000</MaxAgeSeconds>
    <AllowedHeader>*</AllowedHeader>
  </CORSRule>
</CORSConfiguration>
```

## Compute Signature

`FroalaEditor.S3.GetHash` builds an upload policy and signs it with your AWS access key and secret key, using AWS Signature Version 4, which works in every S3 region. Keep the keys in environment variables or a secret store, never in code that reaches the browser.

```csharp
FroalaEditor.S3Config config = new FroalaEditor.S3Config
{
    // The name of your bucket.
    Bucket = "my-bucket",

    // The bucket's region. Leave it out for us-east-1.
    Region = "eu-west-1",

    // The folder for the images. The editor adds the file name right after it.
    KeyStart = "uploads/",

    // Who can read the uploaded images.
    Acl = "public-read",

    // Minutes the signature stays valid. 5 if you leave it out.
    Expiration = "30",

    // AWS keys, read from the server's environment.
    AccessKey = Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable("AWS_ACCESS_KEY"),
    SecretKey = Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable("AWS_SECRET_KEY")
};

object s3Hash = FroalaEditor.S3.GetHash(config);
```

The SDK dates the signature with the server's local time and labels it as UTC. Run the signing server on UTC, or a server behind UTC hands out signatures that have already expired.

## Initialize the JavaScript Editor

Fetch the hash from your server, then start the editor with it as `imageUploadToS3`.

```js
fetch('/FroalaApi/S3Signature')
  .then(function (response) {
    return response.json();
  })
  .then(function (s3Hash) {
    new FroalaEditor('#editor', {
      imageUploadToS3: s3Hash
    });
  });
```

The hash holds the bucket, the region, the folder and the signed policy. The access key ID is part of `x-amz-credential`, and the secret key is not in it.

```json
{
  "bucket": "my-bucket",
  "region": "s3-eu-west-1",
  "keyStart": "uploads/",
  "params": {
    "acl": "public-read",
    "policy": "eyJleHBpcmF0aW9uIjoi...",
    "x-amz-algorithm": "AWS4-HMAC-SHA256",
    "x-amz-credential": "AKIA.../20261030/eu-west-1/s3/aws4_request",
    "x-amz-date": "20261030T000000Z",
    "x-amz-signature": "3f9a..."
  }
}
```

The editor reads the hash each time it uploads. In a session that outlasts the signature, fetch a new hash and set it on `editor.opts.imageUploadToS3`.

The editor sends the upload to `https://s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/my-bucket`, built from the hash's region. Only older S3 regions have addresses in that form. For a bucket in a newer region, such as `eu-south-1`, add an `uploadURL` to the hash, which the editor uses in place of the bucket and region.

```js
s3Hash.uploadURL = 'https://my-bucket.s3.eu-south-1.amazonaws.com';
```

## Complete Example

With the bucket and its CORS rule in place, the page and the controller are all you need.

```js
