# .NET Image Server Delete

Delete an image from your server when a user removes or replaces it in the editor, with `FroalaEditor.Image.Delete`.

## How It Works

1. An editor event tells your code that an image was removed or replaced.
2. Your code sends the image's address to the server.
3. The server deletes the image file.

## Intercept Image Being Removed

Removing an image fires `image.beforeRemove` and then `image.removed`. Use `image.removed`, which comes once the image is out of the content. Neither one sends a request to the server.

Replacing an image fires `image.replaced`, whose third argument, `old_src`, is the address of the image that was replaced.

## Send Request to the Server

The editor has no delete request of its own for this, so send one from both events.

```js
// Ask the server to delete an image file.
function deleteImage(src) {
  fetch('/FroalaApi/DeleteImage', {
    method: 'POST',
    headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/x-www-form-urlencoded' },
    body: new URLSearchParams({ src: src })
  })
    .then(function (response) {
      console.log(response.ok ? 'Image was deleted' : 'Image delete problem');
    })
    .catch(function (err) {
      console.log('Image delete problem: ' + err);
    });
}

new FroalaEditor('#editor', {
  imageUploadURL: '/FroalaApi/UploadImage',
  events: {
    // An image was removed from the editor.
    'image.removed': function ($img) {
      deleteImage($img.attr('src'));
    },

    // An image was replaced. old_src is the image it replaced (froala-editor 5.5.0 and later).
    'image.replaced': function ($img, response, old_src) {
      // Replacing through the Files Manager passes no old_src.
      if (old_src) deleteImage(old_src);
    }
  }
});
```

## Delete the Image

The request carries the image's address in the `src` form field. It is the link the upload returned, so pass it to `FroalaEditor.Image.Delete` as it is. The SDK maps it to a file the same way the upload did and deletes the file if it exists.

**ASP.NET Core**

```csharp
// src is "uploads/name.jpg". The SDK puts wwwroot/ back in front of it.
FroalaEditor.Image.Delete(HttpContext.Request.Form["src"]);
```

**ASP.NET MVC**

```csharp
// src is "/Public/name.jpg", mapped with Server.MapPath.
FroalaEditor.Image.Delete(HttpContext.Request.Form["src"]);
```

`Delete` removes whatever path it gets, and the request comes from the browser. Check that `src` points inside your upload folder before you pass it on.

## Complete Example

```js
function deleteImage(src) {
  fetch('/FroalaApi/DeleteImage', {
    method: 'POST',
    headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/x-www-form-urlencoded' },
    body: new URLSearchParams({ src: src })
  })
    .then(function (response) {
      console.log(response.ok ? 'Image was deleted' : 'Image delete problem');
    })
    .catch(function (err) {
      console.log('Image delete problem: ' + err);
    });
}

new FroalaEditor('#editor', {
  imageUploadURL: '/FroalaApi/UploadImage',
  events: {
    'image.removed': function ($img) {
      deleteImage($img.attr('src'));
    },
    'image.replaced': function ($img, response, old_src) {
      // Replacing through the Files Manager passes no old_src.
      if (old_src) deleteImage(old_src);
    }
  }
});
```

**ASP.NET Core**

```csharp
using System;
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc;

public class FroalaApiController : Controller
{
    [HttpPost]
    public IActionResult DeleteImage()
    {
        string src = HttpContext.Request.Form["src"];

        // Only delete files in the upload folder.
        if (src == null || !src.StartsWith("uploads/") || src.Contains(".."))
        {
            return BadRequest();
        }

        try
        {
            FroalaEditor.Image.Delete(src);
            return Json(true);
        }
        catch (Exception e)
        {
            return Json(new { error = e.Message });
        }
    }
}
```

