# .NET Image Server Upload

Store images that users insert in the editor on your own server with `FroalaEditor.Image.Upload`.

## How It Works

1. You give the editor an upload URL when you start it.
2. When a user inserts an image, the editor sends it to that URL.
3. The server stores the image and answers with its link, which the editor puts in the content.

## Initialize the Editor

Set `imageUploadURL` to the address of your upload action.

```js
new FroalaEditor('#editor', {
  imageUploadURL: '/FroalaApi/UploadImage'
});
```

These options shape the request.

| Option | Default | What it does |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `imageUploadParam` | `'file'` | The name of the field that holds the image. It must match the SDK's `Fieldname`, also `"file"` by default. |
| `imageUploadParams` | `{}` | More fields sent with the image. |
| `imageUploadMethod` | `'POST'` | The HTTP method. |
| `imageMaxSize` | 10 MB | The largest image the editor sends, in bytes. |
| `imageAllowedTypes` | `['jpeg', 'jpg', 'png', 'gif', 'webp']` | The image types the editor sends. Check them on the server too, see [Image Validation](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/docs/sdks/dotnet/image-validation/). |

## Receive the Uploaded Image and Store It

Pass the request's `HttpContext` and the folder to store the image in. The SDK finds the image in the request, gives it a unique name, saves it and creates the folder if it's missing.

**ASP.NET Core**

```csharp
object response = FroalaEditor.Image.Upload(HttpContext, "wwwroot/uploads/");
```

The folder is relative to the folder the app runs from. Use one inside `wwwroot`, which ASP.NET Core serves to the browser.

**ASP.NET MVC**

```csharp
object response = FroalaEditor.Image.Upload(System.Web.HttpContext.Current, "/Public/");
```

The folder is relative to the app's root, as `Server.MapPath` reads it.

The app needs write access to the folder, and the folder must be public, or the editor can't show the image. Without options, the SDK accepts gif, jpeg, jpg, png, svg and blob files. [Image Validation](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/docs/sdks/dotnet/image-validation/) shows how to change that.

## Return the Path to the Uploaded Image

`Upload` returns an object with one property, `link`, the folder plus the new name. Send it back as JSON, which is the answer the editor expects.

```csharp
return Json(response);
```

```json
{ "link": "/Public/3f786850e387550fdab836ed7e6dc881de23001b.jpg" }
```

On ASP.NET Core the SDK takes `wwwroot/` out of the link, so `wwwroot/uploads/` gives `uploads/name.jpg`, a link relative to the page. If something goes wrong, `Upload` throws an exception.

## Complete Example

```js
new FroalaEditor('#editor', {
  imageUploadURL: '/FroalaApi/UploadImage'
});
```

**ASP.NET Core**

