# Java Image S3 Upload

## How it works

1. Create a bucket on Amazon S3 and set the CORS for it.
2. Code computes the Amazon S3 signature on the server side.
3. The editor initializes with the `imageUploadToS3` option.
4. Uploaded images go directly to the S3 bucket without passing through the server.

[Jump to Complete Example](#complete-example)

## Create a S3 Bucket

For information on creating a bucket and setting a region, refer to the [Amazon documentation](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/create-bucket-overview.html). If you have any issues creating it, please [contact Amazon](https://aws.amazon.com/contact-us/) for getting it set up.

## Set CORS on the S3 Bucket

Cross-origin resource sharing (CORS) tells Amazon from which domains to accept requests and what kind of requests. For a detailed explanation of how that works, refer to the [Amazon CORS Documentation](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/cors.html).

The following example shows the recommended configuration, remember to replace `ALLOWED_URL` with the URL of the page where you are using editor.

```json
[
  {
    "AllowedOrigins": ["ALLOWED_URL"],
    "AllowedMethods": ["GET", "POST", "PUT"],
    "AllowedHeaders": ["*"],
    "MaxAgeSeconds": 3000
  }
]
```

The S3 console takes the rule as JSON only. The S3 API also takes it as XML.

```xml
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<CORSConfiguration xmlns="http://s3.amazonaws.com/doc/2006-03-01/">
  <CORSRule>
    <AllowedOrigin>ALLOWED_URL</AllowedOrigin>
    <AllowedMethod>GET</AllowedMethod>
    <AllowedMethod>POST</AllowedMethod>
    <AllowedMethod>PUT</AllowedMethod>
    <MaxAgeSeconds>3000</MaxAgeSeconds>
    <AllowedHeader>*</AllowedHeader>
  </CORSRule>
</CORSConfiguration>
```

## Compute Signature

To send uploaded images to S3, it is necessary to compute a signature using the `AWS access key ID` and `AWS secret access key` and provide it together with the upload request. The editor Java SDK comes with methods to compute the S3 signature using the V4 signing algorithm that works with buckets created on any of the S3 regions.

```java
S3Config config = new S3Config();

// The name of your bucket.
config.bucket = "bucket-name";

// S3 region. If you are using the default us-east-1, this can be ignored.
config.region = "eu-west-1";

// The folder where to upload the images, ending in a slash. The editor adds the file name right after it.
config.keyStart = "uploads/";

// File access.
config.acl = "public-read";

// AWS keys, read from the server's environment.
config.accessKey = System.getenv("AWS_ACCESS_KEY");
config.secretKey = System.getenv("AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY");

// Compute the signature.
Map<Object, Object> hash = S3.getHash(config);
```

New buckets have ACLs turned off, and they turn down uploads that ask for an ACL such as `public-read`. Either turn ACLs on for the bucket, or set `acl` to `bucket-owner-full-control` and let the public read the images through a bucket policy.

The signature lasts 5 minutes, a fixed value in the SDK, and the SDK writes that time in the server's local time labelled as UTC. Run the signing server on UTC (for example with the JVM option `-Duser.timezone=UTC`), or on a server behind UTC the signature has expired before it is used. For a page that stays open longer than 5 minutes, fetch a new hash every few minutes and set it on the editor's `opts.imageUploadToS3`.

## Initialize the Javascript editor

To upload images to Amazon S3, set the `imageUploadToS3` option on initialization. The editor Java SDK computes the hash required for this as the response of the `com.froala.editor.S3.getHash` method.

```html
<script>
  fetch('/amazon_hash')
    .then(function (response) {
      return response.json();
    })
    .then(function (data) {
      new FroalaEditor('.selector', {
        imageUploadToS3: data
      });
    });
</script>
```

The editor sends the upload to `https://s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/bucket-name`, built from the region the SDK returns, `s3-eu-west-1`. Only older S3 regions have addresses in that form. For a bucket in a newer region, such as `eu-south-1`, set `data.uploadURL` to the bucket's address, such as `https://bucket-name.s3.eu-south-1.amazonaws.com`, before you start the editor. The editor then uses it in place of the bucket and region.

## Complete Example

```json
[
  {
    "AllowedOrigins": ["ALLOWED_URL"],
    "AllowedMethods": ["GET", "POST", "PUT"],
    "AllowedHeaders": ["*"],
    "MaxAgeSeconds": 3000
  }
]
```

```java
package com.froala.examples.servlets;

import java.io.IOException;
import java.util.Map;

import javax.servlet.ServletException;
import javax.servlet.annotation.WebServlet;
import javax.servlet.http.HttpServlet;
import javax.servlet.http.HttpServletRequest;
import javax.servlet.http.HttpServletResponse;

import com.froala.editor.S3;
import com.froala.editor.s3.S3Config;
import com.google.gson.Gson;

/**
 * Servlet implementation class AmazonHash
 */
@WebServlet("/amazon_hash")
public class AmazonHash extends HttpServlet {
  private static final long serialVersionUID = 1L;

  /**
   * @see HttpServlet#HttpServlet()
   */
  public AmazonHash() {
    super();
  }

  /**
   * @see HttpServlet#doGet(HttpServletRequest request, HttpServletResponse
   *      response)
   */
  @Override
  protected void doGet(HttpServletRequest request, HttpServletResponse response)
      throws ServletException, IOException {

    Map<Object, Object> hash;
    S3Config config = new S3Config();

    // The name of your bucket.
    config.bucket = "bucket-name";

    // S3 region. If you are using the default us-east-1, this can be ignored.
    config.region = "eu-west-1";

    // The folder where to upload the images, ending in a slash. The editor adds the file name right after it.
    config.keyStart = "uploads/";

    // File access.
    config.acl = "public-read";

    // AWS keys, read from the server's environment.
    config.accessKey = System.getenv("AWS_ACCESS_KEY");
    config.secretKey = System.getenv("AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY");

    try {
      // Compute the signature.
      hash = S3.getHash(config);
    } catch (Exception e) {
      e.printStackTrace();
      response.sendError(HttpServletResponse.SC_INTERNAL_SERVER_ERROR);
      return;
    }

    String jsonResponseData = new Gson().toJson(hash);
    response.setContentType("application/json");
    response.setCharacterEncoding("UTF-8");
    response.getWriter().write(jsonResponseData);
  }
}
