# Node.js Collaboration Overview

The Node.js SDK includes an optional backend for the editor's real-time collaboration plugin. It is split into four modules you compose as needed, a WebSocket relay for live peer sync, plus REST + SQLite stores for suggestions & comments, version snapshots, and offline saves. All four can share a single HTTP server and a single SQLite file.

| Module | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| `Collaborative` | Pure WebSocket relay. Groups clients by `docId` and broadcasts messages between peers. |
| `CollabPersistence` | REST + SQLite for track-change **suggestions** and inline **comments**. |
| `VersionControl` | REST + SQLite for named or automatic content **snapshots**. |
| `AsyncSave` | REST + SQLite for saving/loading the latest content in **offline (async) mode**. |

## Prerequisites

- **Node.js** 22 or later. The SDK's `better-sqlite3` dependency needs Node.js 20 or later, and Node.js 20 is out of support since April 2026.
- **Express**, the `CollabPersistence`, `VersionControl`, and `AsyncSave` modules register their routes on an Express app.
- **better-sqlite3**, required by the three persistence modules. It ships as a regular dependency of the SDK and installs automatically with `npm install`; no separate step is needed.
- **Client side**, the editor's collaboration plugin manages all CRDT/sync state with **Yjs** and connects over a standard WebSocket. The server is a transparent relay and never parses message content.

The three persistence modules can share one SQLite file (default `collab.db`), so a full setup needs just one HTTP server and one database.

## Quick start

A single Express-wrapped HTTP server hosts the WebSocket relay and all three REST stores, backed by one shared SQLite file.

```js
const http    = require('http');
const express = require('express');
const path    = require('path');
const {
  Collaborative,
  CollabPersistence,
  VersionControl,
  AsyncSave,
} = require('wysiwyg-editor-node-sdk');

const app = express();
// The collaboration plugin sends JSON; the save plugin posts form-encoded content.
app.use(express.json({ limit: '5mb' }));
app.use(express.urlencoded({ extended: false, limit: '5mb' }));

// One SQLite file shared by all three persistence modules
const dbPath = path.join(__dirname, 'collab.db');
CollabPersistence.attachRoutes(app, { dbPath });
VersionControl.attachRoutes(app, { dbPath });
AsyncSave.attachRoutes(app, { dbPath });

// Optional: expose live relay stats
app.get('/health', (req, res) => res.json(Collaborative.getStats()));

// Share ONE HTTP server between REST and the WebSocket relay
const server = http.createServer(app);
Collaborative.attachToServer(server);

server.listen(3000, () => {
  console.log('Collaborative backend on http://localhost:3000');
});
```

Use `Collaborative.attachToServer(server)` whenever the REST routes and the relay must share a port. Use `Collaborative.createServer({ port })` only when the relay is the sole service on a port.

### URL conventions

```js
WebSocket : ws://<host>/{docId}
REST base : http://<host>/collab/{docId}/{resource}
```

The `{docId}` used by the WebSocket connection must match the `:docId` in the REST routes. Document IDs are arbitrary strings; URL-encode any characters outside `a-z 0-9 - _`.

## Connect the editor to the backend

Point the editor at the backend in the plugin config.

### Real-time mode

Set the WebSocket URL to the server's address. The editor adds `/{docId}` to it when it connects.

```js
new FroalaEditor('#editor', {
  collabConfig: {
    // Who you are. Other people see this name on your cursor.
    // Leave id out and one gets generated for you — but then you're
    // a different person every refresh, so set it.
    user: {
      id: 'user-1',
      name: 'Alice',
      role: 'editor'          // 'editor' | 'suggester' | 'viewer'
    },
    // WHICH document — think of it as the room number.
    // This MUST match the docId in every URL below.
    docId: 'my-doc-2024',
    // THE LIVE CHANNEL
    // Set this and everything syncs instantly between tabs.
    // Remove it and the editor drops to offline/async mode.
    // The editor connects to ws://localhost:3000/my-doc-2024.
    realTime: {
      syncUrl: 'ws://localhost:3000'
    },
    // WHERE COMMENTS ARE STORED. Without this, comments only exist
    // while someone is connected — they vanish on the last disconnect.
    commentsUrl: 'http://localhost:3000/collab/my-doc-2024/comments',
    // Same idea for track-change suggestions.
    suggestionsUrl: 'http://localhost:3000/collab/my-doc-2024/suggestions',
    // Named snapshots of the document over time.
    versionControl: {
      url: 'http://localhost:3000/collab/my-doc-2024/versions'
    }
  },
  // Offline mode only: where the editor POSTs the document body.
  // Matches AsyncSave's POST /collab/:docId/content
  saveURL: 'http://localhost:3000/collab/my-doc-2024/content'
});
```

### Async (offline) mode

Omit `syncUrl`. The editor then persists through the AsyncSave endpoints instead of syncing live. The save plugin posts the content under the field name `body` (its default `saveParam`), which matches what `POST /collab/:docId/content` expects.

Mounting these routes creates the REST endpoints. To actually use them, point the editor at each one via `collabConfig.commentsUrl`, `collabConfig.suggestionsUrl`, and `collabConfig.versionControl.url`.

For the complete endpoint and method reference, see the [Collaborative Backend API Reference.](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/docs/plugins/collaborative-plugin/collaborative-backend-api-reference/)

# Node.js File S3 Upload

Send the files users insert straight from the browser to an Amazon S3 bucket. Your Node.js server only signs the upload, with `S3.getHash`, and your secret key stays on it.

## How It Works

1. You create a bucket on Amazon S3 and set its CORS rules.
2. Your server computes a signature for the upload.
3. You start the editor with the signature in `fileUploadToS3`.
4. The editor uploads each file straight to the bucket, without passing it through your server.

## Create an S3 Bucket

Create the bucket and choose its region, as [Amazon's guide](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/create-bucket-overview.html) shows. For help with your account, [contact Amazon](https://aws.amazon.com/contact-us/).

A new bucket has ACLs turned off and blocks public access, so S3 refuses an upload whose `acl` is `public-read`. Either allow ACLs and public ACLs on the bucket, or set `acl` to `bucket-owner-full-control` and make the folder readable another way, such as a [bucket policy](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/example-bucket-policies.html).

## Set CORS on the S3 Bucket

CORS rules tell Amazon which sites may send requests to the bucket, and which kinds. Add this rule in the bucket's Permissions tab; the S3 console takes it as JSON. Replace `ALLOWED_URL` with the address of the site that runs the editor. Amazon's [CORS guide](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/cors.html) explains each field.

```json
[
  {
    "AllowedOrigins": ["ALLOWED_URL"],
    "AllowedMethods": ["GET", "POST", "PUT"],
    "AllowedHeaders": ["*"],
    "MaxAgeSeconds": 3000
  }
]
```

## Compute Signature

The upload needs a signature made with your AWS access key ID and secret access key. `S3.getHash` makes it with Amazon's Signature Version 4 and returns the hash the editor needs.

```js
const s3Hash = FroalaEditor.S3.getHash({
  // The name of your bucket.
  bucket: process.env.AWS_BUCKET,
  // The bucket's region. Leave it out for us-east-1.
  region: process.env.AWS_REGION,
  // The folder to upload into. The editor adds a timestamp and the file name.
  keyStart: 'uploads/',
  // Who may read the uploaded files.
  acl: 'public-read',
  // Your AWS keys, kept on the server.
  accessKey: process.env.AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID,
  secretKey: process.env.AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY
});
```

The hash holds the bucket, the region, the folder and the signed policy, with your access key ID in it. It never holds the secret key. Anyone who gets a hash can upload to that folder until it expires, so serve it only to people who may use the editor.

[Node.js SDK S3 Reference](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/docs/sdks/nodejs/references/s3/)

## Initialize the Editor

Fetch the hash from your server and pass it to the editor as `fileUploadToS3`.

```js
fetch('/get_signature')
  .then(function (response) { return response.json(); })
  .then(function (s3Hash) {
    new FroalaEditor('#editor', {
      fileUploadToS3: s3Hash
    });
  });
```

### Signature Lifetime

The policy `getHash` signs expires five minutes after it is made. If the page stays open longer, fetch a new hash before then and give it to the editor, which reads the option at each upload.

```js
function getSignature() {
  return fetch('/get_signature').then(function (response) { return response.json(); });
}

getSignature().then(function (s3Hash) {
  const editor = new FroalaEditor('#editor', { fileUploadToS3: s3Hash });

  // Every four minutes, give the editor a fresh signature.
  setInterval(function () {
    getSignature().then(function (fresh) { editor.opts.fileUploadToS3 = fresh; });
  }, 4 * 60 * 1000);
});
```

### Regions

`getHash` returns the region as `s3-<region>`, and the editor uploads to `https://s3-<region>.amazonaws.com/<bucket>`.

Only some older regions have that address. For a newer region, add an `uploadURL`, which the editor uses in place of the bucket and region.

```js
// On the server, after getHash.
s3Hash.uploadURL = 'https://s3.' + process.env.AWS_REGION + '.amazonaws.com/' + process.env.AWS_BUCKET;
```

## Complete Example

The CORS rule on the bucket.

```json
[
  {
    "AllowedOrigins": ["ALLOWED_URL"],
    "AllowedMethods": ["GET", "POST", "PUT"],
    "AllowedHeaders": ["*"],
    "MaxAgeSeconds": 3000
  }
]
```

The server, which signs the upload.

```js
const express = require('express');
const FroalaEditor = require('wysiwyg-editor-node-sdk');

const app = express();

app.get('/get_signature', function (req, res) {
  const s3Hash = FroalaEditor.S3.getHash({
    bucket: process.env.AWS_BUCKET,
    region: process.env.AWS_REGION,
    keyStart: 'uploads/',
    acl: 'public-read',
    accessKey: process.env.AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID,
    secretKey: process.env.AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY
  });
  res.json(s3Hash);
});

app.listen(3000);
```

The page, which starts the editor.

```js
fetch('/get_signature')
  .then(function (response) { return response.json(); })
  .then(function (s3Hash) {
    new FroalaEditor('#editor', {
      fileUploadToS3: s3Hash
    });
  });
```

