# Node.js Image Server Delete

Delete an image's file from your Node.js server when a user removes or replaces the image in the editor, with `Image.delete`.

## How It Works

1. Your code listens for an image being removed or replaced in the editor.
2. It sends the image's path to your server.
3. The server deletes the file.

## Intercept Image Being Removed

| Event | When it fires |
| --- | --- |
| [`image.beforeRemove($img)`](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/docs/events/#image.beforeRemove) | After the user confirms, before the image leaves the content. Returning `false` keeps it. |
| [`image.removed($img)`](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/docs/events/#image.removed) | After the image is gone from the content. Use this one to delete the file. |
| [`image.replaced($img, response, old_src)`](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/docs/events/#image.replaced) | After a new image replaces one. `old_src` is the path of the image it replaced. |

The editor only removes the image from the content. It makes no request to your server.

## Send Request to the Server

Post the image's path from the event handler. Inside a handler, `this` is the editor.

```js
// Ask the server to delete an image file.
function deleteImage(src) {
  fetch('/delete_image', {
    method: 'POST',
    body: new URLSearchParams({ src: src })
  }).then(function (response) {
    console.log(response.ok ? 'Image deleted' : 'Image delete problem');
  });
}

new FroalaEditor('#editor', {
  events: {
    'image.removed': function ($img) {
      deleteImage($img.attr('src'));
    },
    'image.replaced': function ($img, response, old_src) {
      // Replacing through the Files Manager passes no old_src.
      if (old_src) deleteImage(old_src);
    }
  }
});
```

## Delete the Image

The path arrives as `req.body.src` once a form body parser runs. `Image.delete` deletes it, relative to the folder of your app's main file, and calls back with an error if it can't.

```js
FroalaEditor.Image.delete(req.body.src, function (err) {
  // ...
});
```

The SDK deletes whatever path it gets, without checking it. Check that the path is inside your uploads folder first, as the example below does, or a request could delete other files.

## Complete Example

```js
function deleteImage(src) {
  fetch('/delete_image', {
    method: 'POST',
    body: new URLSearchParams({ src: src })
  }).then(function (response) {
    console.log(response.ok ? 'Image deleted' : 'Image delete problem');
  });
}

new FroalaEditor('#editor', {
  events: {
    'image.removed': function ($img) {
      deleteImage($img.attr('src'));
    },
    'image.replaced': function ($img, response, old_src) {
      // Replacing through the Files Manager passes no old_src.
      if (old_src) deleteImage(old_src);
    }
  }
});
```

```js
const express = require('express');
const FroalaEditor = require('wysiwyg-editor-node-sdk');

const app = express();

// Read form posts into req.body.
app.use(express.urlencoded({ extended: false }));

app.post('/delete_image', function (req, res) {
  const src = req.body.src;

  // Delete only what is inside the uploads folder.
  if (typeof src !== 'string' || !src.startsWith('/uploads/') || src.includes('..')) {
    return res.status(400).end();
  }

  FroalaEditor.Image.delete(src, function (err) {
    if (err) {
      return res.status(404).json({ error: String(err) });
    }
    res.end();
  });
});

app.listen(3000);
```

