# Node.js Image Server Upload

Store the images users insert in the editor on your Node.js server with `Image.upload`.

## How It Works

1. You set the upload options when you start the editor.
2. When a user inserts an image, the editor sends it to your server.
3. The server stores the image and sends back its link, and the editor shows it.

## Initialize the Editor

Set `imageUploadURL` to the route that receives the images.

```js
new FroalaEditor('#editor', {
  imageUploadURL: '/upload_image'
});
```

These options shape the request.

| Option | Default | What it does |
| --- | --- | --- |
| [`imageUploadURL`](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/docs/options/#imageUploadURL) | `null` | Where the editor sends the image. |
| [`imageUploadParam`](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/docs/options/#imageUploadParam) | `'file'` | The name of the form field that holds the image. The SDK reads `file`, so change both or neither. |
| [`imageUploadParams`](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/docs/options/#imageUploadParams) | `{}` | More fields to send with the image. |
| [`imageUploadMethod`](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/docs/options/#imageUploadMethod) | `'POST'` | The HTTP method. |
| [`imageMaxSize`](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/docs/options/#imageMaxSize) | 10 MB | The largest image the editor sends, checked in the browser. |
| [`imageAllowedTypes`](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/docs/options/#imageAllowedTypes) | `['jpeg', 'jpg', 'png', 'gif', 'webp']` | The image types the editor sends, checked in the browser. |

## Receive the Uploaded Image and Store It

The SDK reads the image from the request and saves it under a random name in the folder you give it. The folder is relative to the folder of your app's main file, and it must exist and be writable.

```js
FroalaEditor.Image.upload(req, '/uploads/', function (err, data) {
  // ...
});
```

Serve that folder too, so the browser can load the images.

By default the SDK accepts GIF, JPEG, PNG and SVG images, and pasted images named `blob`. WebP isn't on its list, though the editor sends it, so a WebP image is refused. To change the list, see [Image Validation](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/docs/sdks/nodejs/image-validation/).

## Return the Path to the Uploaded Image

When the image is saved, the callback gets `{ link }`, the folder plus the new name, such as `/uploads/1f0e3d.jpg`. Send it back as JSON; the editor reads `link` and inserts the image.

On an error, answer with a status outside 200 to 299. The editor then shows an upload error and fires [`image.error`](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/docs/events/#image.error).

```js
if (err) {
  return res.status(400).json({ error: String(err) });
}
res.json(data);
```

## Complete Example

```js
new FroalaEditor('#editor', {
  imageUploadURL: '/upload_image'
});
```

```js
const path = require('path');
const fs = require('fs');
const express = require('express');
const FroalaEditor = require('wysiwyg-editor-node-sdk');

const app = express();

// The SDK saves into this folder, next to the app's main file.
fs.mkdirSync(path.join(__dirname, 'uploads'), { recursive: true });

// Serve the uploads, so the links the SDK returns load in the browser.
app.use('/uploads', express.static(path.join(__dirname, 'uploads')));

app.post('/upload_image', function (req, res) {
  FroalaEditor.Image.upload(req, '/uploads/', function (err, data) {
    if (err) {
      return res.status(400).json({ error: String(err) });
    }
    res.json(data);
  });
});

app.listen(3000);
```

