# PHP File Server Delete

## How it works

1. Code intercepts the file removed from the editor.
2. Removing a file sends to the server a request to delete the file from disk.
3. Once the request reaches the server, it deletes the file.

[Jump to Complete Example](#complete-example)

## Intercept file being removed

Removing a file from the editor triggers the `file.unlink` event, once the file link is gone from the editable area. Its handler receives the removed link, so you can use it to delete the file from the server.

## Send request to the server

There is no built-in feature that makes the request to the server, however you can use an AJAX request.

```html
<script>
  // Ask the server to delete a file.
  function deleteFile (src) {
    fetch('/delete_file.php', {
      method: 'POST',
      headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/x-www-form-urlencoded' },
      body: new URLSearchParams({ src: src })
    })
    .then(function (response) {
      console.log(response.ok ? 'File was deleted' : 'File delete problem');
    })
    .catch(function (err) {
      console.log('File delete problem: ' + err);
    });
  }

  new FroalaEditor('.selector', {
    events: {
      // Catch the file being removed. link is the removed file link.
      'file.unlink': function (link) {
        deleteFile(link.getAttribute('href'));
      }
    }
  });
</script>
```

## Delete the file

The server implementation is responsible for receiving the request and handling it appropriately. Using the code from the previous step, makes the file path available in the `$_POST['src']` variable. The `FroalaEditor_File::delete()` method from the PHP SDK expects the path of the file to remove from disk.

The path of the file is relative to the `$_SERVER['DOCUMENT_ROOT']` global variable.

```php
$response = FroalaEditor_File::delete($_POST['src']);
```

