# Python File Server Delete

## How it works

1. Code intercepts the file removed from the editor.
2. Removing a file sends to the server a request to delete the file from disk.
3. Once the request reaches the server, it deletes the file.

[Jump to Complete Example](#complete-example)

## Intercept file being removed

Removing a file from the editor triggers the `file.unlink` event. You can use it to delete the file from the server, since at that point you make sure that the file was removed from the editable area.

## Send request to the server

There is no built-in feature that makes the request to the server, however you can use an AJAX request.

```html
<script>
  // Catch the file being removed.
  new FroalaEditor('.selector', {
    events: {
      'file.unlink': function (link) {
        // Request URL.
        fetch('/delete_file', {
          // Request method.
          method: 'POST',

          // Request params.
          body: new URLSearchParams({ src: link.getAttribute('href') })
        })
        .then(function (response) {
          console.log(response.ok ? 'File was deleted' : 'File delete problem');
        })
        .catch(function (err) {
          console.log('File delete problem: ' + err);
        });
      }
    }
  });
</script>
```

## Delete the file

The server implementation is responsible for receiving the request and handling it appropriately. The code on the previous step makes the file path available in the body of the request, under the `src` key. The `File.delete` method from the Python SDK expects the path of the file to remove from disk.

The SDK joins the path to the folder of the script that started the server, as [Where Files Go](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/docs/sdks/python/#where-files-go) explains. It deletes whatever path it gets without checking it, so check that the path is inside your uploads folder first.

```python
File.delete(src)
```

