# Ruby File Server Delete

## How it works

1. Code intercepts the file removed from the editor.
2. Removing a file sends to the server a request to delete the file from disk.
3. Once the request reaches the server, it deletes the file.

[Jump to Complete Example](#complete-example)

## Intercept file being removed

Removing a file from the editor triggers the `file.unlink` event, once the file's link is gone from the editable area. Use it to delete the file from the server.

## Send request to the server

There is no built-in feature that makes the request to the server, however you can use an AJAX request.

```html
<script>
// Ask the server to delete a file.
function deleteFile (src) {
    fetch('/delete_file', {
    method: 'POST',
    headers: {
        'Content-Type': 'application/x-www-form-urlencoded',
        // Rails checks the CSRF token on every POST.
        'X-CSRF-Token': document.querySelector('meta[name="csrf-token"]').content
    },
    body: new URLSearchParams({ src: src })
    })
    .then(function (response) {
    console.log(response.ok ? 'File was deleted' : 'File delete problem');
    })
    .catch(function (err) {
    console.log('File delete problem: ' + err);
    });
}

new FroalaEditor('.selector', {
    events: {
    // Catch the file being removed.
    'file.unlink': function (link) {
        deleteFile(link.getAttribute('href'));
    }
    }
});
</script>
```

## Delete the file

The server implementation is responsible for receiving the request and handling it appropriately. The code on the previous step makes the file path available in the `params` variable. The `FroalaEditorSDK::File.delete()` method from the [Ruby SDK](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/docs/sdks/ruby/) expects the path of the file and the folder to remove it from.

The path of the folder is relative to the `Rails.root` global variable.

```ruby
FroalaEditorSDK::File.delete(params[:src], "public/uploads/files/")
```

