# Ruby Image Server Delete

## How it works

1. Code intercepts the image removed or replaced in the editor.
2. Removing an image sends to the server a request to delete the image from disk.
3. Once the request reaches the server, it deletes the image.

[Jump to Complete Example](#complete-example)

## Intercept image being removed

Removing an image from the editor triggers two events, `image.beforeRemove` and `image.removed`. You can use either of them to delete the image from the server, however it is recommended to use the second one, since at that point you make sure that the image was removed from the editable area.

Replacing an image triggers `image.replaced`. It passes the previous image's `src` as a third argument, `old_src`, so you can delete the replaced file the same way.

## Send request to the server

There is no built-in feature that makes the request to the server, however you can use an AJAX request.

```html
<script>
// Ask the server to delete an image file.
function deleteImage (src) {
    fetch('/delete_image', {
    method: 'POST',
    headers: {
        'Content-Type': 'application/x-www-form-urlencoded',
        // Rails checks the CSRF token on every POST.
        'X-CSRF-Token': document.querySelector('meta[name="csrf-token"]').content
    },
    body: new URLSearchParams({ src: src })
    })
    .then(function (response) {
    console.log(response.ok ? 'Image was deleted' : 'Image delete problem');
    })
    .catch(function (err) {
    console.log('Image delete problem: ' + err);
    });
}

new FroalaEditor('.selector', {
    events: {
    // An image was removed from the editor.
    'image.removed': function ($img) {
        deleteImage($img.attr('src'));
    },

    // An image was replaced. old_src is the image that was replaced (v5.5+).
    'image.replaced': function ($img, response, old_src) {
        // Replacing through the Files Manager passes no old_src.
        if (old_src) deleteImage(old_src);
    }
    }
});
</script>
```

## Delete the image

The server implementation is responsible for receiving the request and handling it appropriately. The code on the previous step makes the image path available in the `params[:src]` variable. The `FroalaEditorSDK::Image.delete(params[:src], "public/uploads/images/")` method from the Ruby SDK expects the path of the image and the folder to remove it from. It deletes the file with that name from the folder.

The path of the folder is relative to the `Rails.root` global variable.

```ruby
render :json => FroalaEditorSDK::Image.delete(params[:src], "public/uploads/images/")
```

